General elections were held in [[Socialist Republic of Serbia|Serbia]], a constituent federal unit of [[Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia|SFR Yugoslavia]], in December 1990 to elect the [[president of Serbia]] and [[Member of parliament|members]] of the [[National Assembly (Serbia)|National Assembly]]. The presidential election and the first round of the parliamentary elections were held on 9 December, with the second round of the parliamentary elections taking place on 23 December. The elections were scheduled after the ratification of a new [[Constitution of Serbia|constitution]] on 28 September, which was approved by voters in a [[1990 Serbian constitutional referendum|referendum]] held in July. These were Serbia's first [[multi-party elections]], and the only parliamentary election to be held using a [[Two-round system|two-round voting system]] with single-member [[constituencies]]; all future elections used [[proportional representation]].

[[Slobodan Milošević]] came to power in Serbia at the [[8th session of the Central Committee of the League of Communists of Serbia|8th session]] in 1987. He then led the [[anti-bureaucratic revolution]], which saw his supporters overthrow the leadership of Montenegro, Kosovo, and Vojvodina. After the July 1990 referendum, Milošević's [[Socialist Party of Serbia]] (SPS), [[Vuk Drašković]]'s [[Serbian Renewal Movement]] (SPO), and [[Dragoljub Mićunović]]'s [[Democratic Party (Serbia)|Democratic Party]] (DS) became registered parties. Due to low requirements, 1,701 candidates ran for parliament and 32 for president. The campaign was marked by intense [[Political polarisation|polarisation]] and politically driven incidents. SPS inherited a significant amount of political infrastructure upon its formation, giving it an immeasurable advantage over the opposition. Furthermore, SPS controlled most television, radio, and newspapers, which attacked and discredited opponents, especially Drašković, but portrayed SPS as the party of peace. The SPO and [[Ivan Đurić]] of [[Union of Reform Forces of Yugoslavia|Union of Reform Forces]] during the campaign called for an [[election boycott]] over but ultimately dropped after the government agreed to most of their demands in late November. 

The primary issue during the campaign was [[Serbian nationalism|nationalism]]. SPS and Milošević focused on positive themes and stability, whereas Drašković ran on a nationalist platform. DS and Đurić campaigned on a [[Liberalism|liberal]] programme. By the end of the campaign, an SPS supporter had murdered an SPO activist. With a [[Voter turnout|turnout]] of 71%, primarily due to the [[Kosovo Albanians]]' boycott, Milošević won the presidential election in the first round in a [[Landslide victory|landslide]], with Drašković placing second. Despite gaining 48% of the popular vote, SPS received 194 out of 250 seats in the National Assembly due to the first-past-the-post electoral system.

The opposition initially claimed [[electoral fraud]] in the elections, but eventually conceded. Observers and political scientists reported that electoral irregularities such as ballot stuffing and [[vote buying]] took place. After the elections, [[Dragutin Zelenović]] became prime minister. His tenure saw Drašković's detention during the [[1991 protests in Belgrade|1991 mass protests in Belgrade]], the outbreak of the [[Yugoslav Wars]], and the establishment of [[Federal Republic of Yugoslavia]] in 1992. [[1992 Serbian general election|Snap elections]] were then called for December 1992.{{TOC limit|3}}

== Background ==
=== Post-World War II ===
[[File:Slobodan Milošević i Ivan Stambolić, 1986.jpg|thumb|alt=Slobodan Milošević talking to Ivan Stambolić during the 10th Congress of the League of Communists of Serbia in May 1986|[[Slobodan Milošević]] (left) came to power in 1987 after removing [[Ivan Stambolić]] (right) and his allies from key positions]]
After [[World War II]], the [[League of Communists of Yugoslavia|Communist Party]] (later the League of Communists of Yugoslavia, SKJ)<ref>{{Cite book |last=Biondich |first=Mark |author-link=Mark Biondich |title=The Balkans: Revolution, War, and Political Violence Since 1878 |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]] |year=2011 |isbn=978-0-19-929905-8 |location=Oxford, United Kingdom |pages=180}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Centrih |first=Lev |title=The Road to Collapse: The Demise of the League of Communists of Yugoslavia |publisher=[[Rosa Luxemburg Foundation|Rosa Luxemburg Foundation Southeast Europe]] |year=2014 |isbn=978-86-88745-13-0 |pages=13}}</ref> consolidated power in [[Yugoslavia]], transforming the country into a [[socialist state]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Lampe |first=John R. |author-link=John R. Lampe |title=Yugoslavia as History: Twice There Was a Country |publisher=[[Cambridge University Press]] |year=2010 |isbn=978-0-521-77357-7 |edition=2 |location=Cambridge, United Kingdom |pages=233}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Čalić |first=Marie–Janine |title=A History of Yugoslavia |publisher=[[Purdue University Press]] |year=2019 |isbn=978-1-61249-564-4 |edition=1 |location=West Lafayette, Indiana |pages=154, 252}}</ref> Each [[Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia#Federal units|constituent republic]] had its own branch of the Communist Party, with [[Socialist Republic of Serbia|Serbia]] having the [[Communist Party of Serbia]],<ref>{{Cite book |last=Banac |first=Ivo |author-link=Ivo Banac |title=With Stalin against Tito: Cominformist Splits in Yugoslav Communism |publisher=[[Cornell University Press]] |year=1988 |isbn=978-1-5017-2083-3 |location=Ithaca, New York |pages=110}}</ref> later renamed the League of Communists of Serbia (SKS).<ref>{{Cite book |last=Woodward |first=Susan L. |author-link=Susan L. Woodward |title=Socialist Unemployment: The Political Economy of Yugoslavia, 1945–1990 |publisher=[[Princeton University Press]] |year=1995 |isbn=978-0-691-08645-3 |edition=1 |location=Princeton, New Jersey |pages=182}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Derbyshire |first1=Denis J. |title=Encyclopedia of World Political Systems |last2=Derbyshire |first2=Ian |publisher=[[Taylor & Francis]] |year=2000 |isbn=978-1-317-47156-1 |edition=1 |location=Armonk, New York |pages=456}}</ref> With the [[death of Josip Broz Tito]], the leader of Yugoslavia, in 1980, the country was faced with economic issues, constitutional problems, and a rise in [[ethnic nationalism]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Haass |first=Richard |author-link=Richard N. Haass |title=Economic Sanctions and American Diplomacy |publisher=[[Council on Foreign Relations]] |year=1998 |isbn=978-0-87609-212-5 |pages=179}}</ref>

Within Serbia, [[Slobodan Milošević]] came to power at the [[8th session of the Central Committee of the League of Communists of Serbia|8th session]] in September 1987.<ref name=":10">{{Cite web |last=Georgievski |first=Jovana |date=25 August 2020 |title="Fatalni zaokret": Od najbližeg saradnika do poslednje žrtve režima Slobodana Miloševića |trans-title="Fatal u-turn": From the closest collaborator to the last victim of Slobodan Milošević's regime |url=https://www.bbc.com/serbian/lat/srbija-53889035 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230811043015/https://www.bbc.com/serbian/lat/srbija-53889035 |archive-date=11 August 2023 |access-date=10 August 2023 |website=[[BBC News]] |language=sr}}</ref><ref name=":12">{{Cite book |last=Grdešić |first=Marko |title=The Shape of Populism: Serbia Before the Dissolution of Yugoslavia |publisher=[[University of Michigan Press]] |year=2019 |isbn=978-0-472-13133-4 |location=Ann Arbor, Michigan}}</ref>{{Rp|page=36}} Milošević served as the president of the [[City Committee of the League of Communists of Belgrade|League of Communists of Belgrade City Committee]] until being appointed president of SKS in May 1986 upon the proposal of his mentor [[Ivan Stambolić]], a reformist within SKS.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |date=2002 |title=O Ivanu Stamboliću |trans-title=About Ivan Stambolić |url=http://www.b92.net/specijal/stambolic/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810230604/http://www.b92.net/specijal/stambolic/ |archive-date=10 August 2023 |access-date=10 August 2023 |website=[[B92]] |language=sr}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite news |last=Traynor |first=Ian |date=1 April 2003 |title=Ivan Stambolic |url=https://www.theguardian.com/news/2003/apr/01/guardianobituaries.balkans |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200620082840/https://www.theguardian.com/news/2003/apr/01/guardianobituaries.balkans |archive-date=20 June 2020 |access-date=13 August 2023 |work=[[The Guardian]] |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Milošević turned [[populist]] in April 1987 and then became a critic of Stambolić.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last1=Howard |first1=Marc Morje |title=World Order After Leninism |last2=Sil |first2=Rudra |last3=Tismaneanu |first3=Vladimir |publisher=[[University of Washington Press]] |year=2007 |isbn=978-0-295-98628-9 |pages=117}}</ref> Milošević dismissed Stambolić's allies at the 8th session, and in December 1987, Stambolić was removed as president of Serbia.<ref name=":10" /><ref name=":4">{{Cite news |last=Mihovilović |first=Maroje |date=2 April 2003 |title=Milošević's greatest betrayal: How Milošević killed his best friend |url=https://arhiva.nacional.hr/en/clanak/18198/how-milosevic-killed-his-best-friend |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810232341/https://arhiva.nacional.hr/en/clanak/18198/how-milosevic-killed-his-best-friend |archive-date=10 August 2023 |access-date=10 August 2023 |work=[[Nacional (weekly)|Nacional]]}}</ref> With Milošević now in power, protests supporting Milošević's policies, known as the [[anti-bureaucratic revolution]], started in Serbia and [[Socialist Republic of Montenegro|Montenegro]] in 1988.<ref name=":12" />{{Rp|page=41}}<ref name=":2">{{Cite book |last=Biondich |first=Mark |title=The Balkans: Revolution, War, and Political Violence since 1878 |publisher=[[OUP Oxford]] |year=2011 |isbn=978-0-19-155951-8 |pages=200}}</ref> Following the protests, the leadership in Montenegro, Vojvodina, and Kosovo was replaced by the pro-Milošević faction.<ref name=":2" /><ref>{{Cite book |last=Sombatpoonsiri |first=Janjira |title=Humor and Nonviolent Struggle in Serbia |publisher=[[Syracuse University Press]] |year=2015 |isbn=978-0-8156-5340-0 |pages=195}}</ref>

Milošević was named [[President of the Presidency of SR Serbia|president of the presidency of the Socialist Republic of Serbia]] in May 1989, after being nominated to the position by the [[Socialist Alliance of Working People of Serbia]].<ref name="Martinov2000">{{cite book |last1=Martinov |first1=Zlatoje |author-link=Zlatoje Martinov |title=U podnožju demokratskih propileja: Izbori u Srbiji, 1990–2000 |date=2000 |publisher=[[Republika (Serbian magazine)|Republika]] |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=At the pedestal of the democratic propylaea: Elections in Serbia, 1990–2000 |oclc=50410508}}</ref>{{Rp|page=15}}<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Hassenstab |first1=Christine M. |title=Central and Southeast European Politics since 1989 |last2=Ramet |first2=Sabrina P. |publisher=[[Cambridge University Press]] |year=2019 |isbn=978-1-108-49991-0 |pages=333}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Judah |first=Tim |author-link=Tim Judah |title=The Serbs: History, Myth, and the Destruction of Yugoslavia |publisher=[[Yale University Press]] |year=1997 |isbn=978-0-300-07113-9 |location=New Haven, Connecticut |pages=163}}</ref> In response to his appointment, [[1989 Serbian general election|general elections]] were announced for November 1989.<ref name="Martinov2000" />{{Rp|page=15}}<ref name="dvaveka">{{Cite book |last=Vukmirović |first=Dragan |url=https://pod2.stat.gov.rs/objavljenepublikacije/g2008/pdf/g20082002.pdf |title=Dva veka razvoja Srbije: statistički pregled |publisher=[[Republic Bureau of Statistics]] |year=2008 |isbn=978-86-84433-80-2 |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=Two centuries of Serbia's development: a statistical overview |access-date=15 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230812133721/https://pod2.stat.gov.rs/objavljenepublikacije/g2008/pdf/g20082002.pdf |archive-date=12 August 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref>{{Rp|page=27}} Milošević won the election in a [[Landslide victory|landslide]], officially being elected president of the presidency of the SR Serbia.<ref name="Martinov2000" />{{Rp|page=19}} These were the last one-party elections in Serbia.<ref name=":15">{{Cite web |last=Belić |first=Nikola |date=11 November 2016 |title=Kobno kašnjenje Beograda dok se rušio Berlinski zid |trans-title=Belgrade's fatal delay while the Berlin Wall was falling |url=https://www.politika.rs/sr/clanak/367643/Kobno-kasnjenje-Beograda-dok-se-rusio-Berlinski-zid |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220418035028/https://www.politika.rs/sr/clanak/367643/Kobno-kasnjenje-Beograda-dok-se-rusio-Berlinski-zid |archive-date=18 April 2022 |access-date=14 August 2023 |website=[[Politika]] |language=sr}}</ref> [[Stanko Radmilović]], a Milošević loyalist, became the [[prime minister of Serbia]] after the elections.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Staar |first=Richard Felix |title=1990 Yearbook on International Communist Affairs |publisher=[[Hoover Institution on War, Revolution, and Peace]] |year=1990 |isbn=978-0-8179-8941-5 |location=California |pages=449}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Vujačić |first=Veljko Marko |title=Communism and Nationalism in Russia and Serbia |publisher=[[University of California]] |year=1995 |volume=2 |pages=388}}</ref>

In January 1990, an [[14th Congress of the League of Communists of Yugoslavia|SKJ extraordinary congress]] was held to address the dispute over Milošević's centralisation reforms and reforms of the [[Assembly of Yugoslavia|Federal Assembly of Yugoslavia]].<ref name=":20">{{Cite journal |last=Pauković |first=Davor |date=2008 |title=Posljednji kongres Saveza komunista Jugoslavije: uzroci, tijek i posljedice raspada |trans-title=The last congress of the League of Communists of Yugoslavia: causes, course, and consequences of the collapse |url=https://hrcak.srce.hr/35202 |url-status=live |journal=Suvremene Teme |volume=1 |issue=1 |issn=1847-2397 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230520151708/https://hrcak.srce.hr/35202 |archive-date=20 May 2023 |access-date=15 August 2023}}</ref>{{Rp|page=|pages=21–22}} During the congress, SKS proposed implementing a "one man–one vote" system, which was opposed by the [[League of Communists of Slovenia|Slovene delegation]], who favoured a [[Confederation|confederated]] Yugoslavia.<ref name=":21">{{Cite web |date=22 January 2020 |title=Šest stvari koje treba da znate o 14. kongresu SKJ |trans-title=Six things you should know about the 14th SKJ Congress |url=https://www.rts.rs/lat/vesti/drustvo/3821253/sest-stvari-koje-treba-da-znate-o-14-kongresu-skj.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230814222811/https://www.rts.rs/lat/vesti/drustvo/3821253/sest-stvari-koje-treba-da-znate-o-14-kongresu-skj.html |archive-date=14 August 2023 |access-date=14 August 2023 |website=[[Radio Television of Serbia]] |language=sr}}</ref> Milošević was fiercely opposed to confederalism.<ref name=":5">{{Cite news |last=Stojanović |first=Dušan |date=19 July 1990 |title=Yugoslavia: a country on the brink of disintegration |url=https://newspaperarchive.com/cumberland-times-news-jul-19-1990-p-7/ |access-date=9 July 2024 |work=[[Cumberland Times-News]], [[Associated Press]] |pages=7 |archive-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709201223/https://newspaperarchive.com/cumberland-times-news-jul-19-1990-p-7/ |url-status=live }}</ref> With the assistance of delegates from Montenegro, Vojvodina, Kosovo, and the [[League of Communists Organisation in the Yugoslav People's Army|Yugoslav People's Army]], the proposals from SKS were accepted, while the Slovene and Bosnian proposals were rejected.<ref name=":21" /><ref name=":23">{{Cite web |last=Tempest |first=Rone |date=23 January 1990 |title=Communists in Yugoslavia Split Into Factions |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1990-01-23-mn-594-story.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201019181308/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1990-01-23-mn-594-story.html |archive-date=19 October 2020 |access-date=14 August 2023 |website=[[Los Angeles Times]]}}</ref> This ultimately led to the dissolution of SKJ shortly after the congress.<ref name=":20" />{{Rp|page=29|pages=}}

=== Constitutional referendum ===
{{Further|1990 Serbian constitutional referendum}}
On 13 June 1990, the [[Democratic Party (Serbia)|Democratic Party]] (DS), [[Liberal Democratic Party (Serbia, 1989)|Liberal Party]] (LS), [[People's Radical Party (1990)|People's Radical Party]] (NRS), [[Serbian Renewal Movement]] (SPO), and [[Social Democratic Party of Yugoslavia (1990)|Social Democratic Party of Yugoslavia]] (SDPJ) organised the first opposition protest since World War II.<ref name=":30" /><ref name=":88" />{{Rp|page=52}} They demanded that free elections be held that year and petitioned for the establishment of a multi-party system.<ref name=":36" /> The protest in Belgrade drew between 30,000 and 40,000 people.<ref name=":36">{{Cite news |date=14 June 1990 |title=Anti-government rallies sweep Balkans; tens of thousands block streets in Yugoslavia, Bulgaria |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/431975037 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[Montreal Gazette]] |pages=A11 |id={{ProQuest|431975037}} |archive-date=11 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240711163504/https://www.proquest.com/docview/431975037 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":35" /><ref name=":37">{{Cite news |last=Fish |first=Jim |date=14 June 1990 |title=Protesters Demand Free Serbian Vote; Tens of Thousands Denounce Ruling Communist Party, President Milosevic |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/307308731 |access-date=11 July 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |pages=A32 |id={{ProQuest|307308731}} |archive-date=29 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240729193730/https://www.proquest.com/docview/307308731 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Radio Television of Belgrade]] (RTB) covered the protest negatively and the government labelled the protest as "anti-Serbian".<ref name=":30" /><ref name=":37" /> During the protest, the [[Police of Serbia|police]] intervened and brutally attacked DS politicians [[Dragoljub Mićunović]] and [[Borislav Pekić]].<ref name=":30" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=14 June 1990 |title=At least 2 die in Bucharest bloodshed; Election disputes erupt into violence in 3 East European nations |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/251746152 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[Edmonton Journal]] |page=A1 |id={{ProQuest|251746152}}}}</ref> Four cars were damaged during the protests, according to the police, and seven demonstrators were arrested, according to the opposition.<ref>{{Cite news |date=14 June 1990 |title=Serbians demand elections: Thousands march in Belgrade streets |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/243509364 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[The Vancouver Sun]], [[Reuters]] |pages=A6 |id={{ProQuest|243509364}} |archive-date=29 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240729193758/https://www.proquest.com/docview/243509364 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Landay |first=Jonathan S. |date=13 June 1990 |title=Belgrade police beat anti-communist protesters at rally |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1990/06/13/Belgrade-police-beat-anti-communist-protesters-at-rally/7823645249600/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[United Press International]]}}</ref>

Milošević then unexpectedly announced on 25 June 1990 in the [[Assembly of the Socialist Republic of Serbia]] that a constitutional referendum would take place on 1 and 2 July, to determine the new [[constitution of Serbia]].<ref name=":6">{{Cite journal |last=Sokolović |first=Zoran |date=25 June 1990 |title=Odluka da se pristupi donošenju novog ustava Socijalističke Republike Srbije i da se raspiše republički referendum radi potvrđivanja ove odluke |trans-title=The decision to proceed with the adoption of a new constitution of the Socialist Republic of Serbia and to call a republican referendum in order to confirm this decision |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6394 |journal=Official Gazette of the Socialist Republic of Serbia |language=sr |volume=29 |pages=516 |issn=0351-5613 |archive-date=29 July 2024 |access-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240729195237/http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6394 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":8">{{Cite news |date=28 June 1990 |title=Yugoslavs rally against media control |url=https://newspaperarchive.com/arlington-heights-daily-herald-suburban-chicago-jun-28-1990-p-8/ |access-date=9 July 2024 |work=[[Daily Herald (Arlington Heights, Illinois)|Daily Herald]], [[United Press International]] |pages=8}}</ref> The referendum was held during a crisis in [[Socialist Autonomous Province of Kosovo|Kosovo]].<ref name=":9">{{Cite news |date=2 July 1990 |title=Serbs vote for second day in referendum |url=https://newspaperarchive.com/delphos-daily-herald-jul-02-1990-p-10/ |access-date=9 July 2024 |work=[[Delphos Daily Herald]], [[United Press International]] |pages=10 |archive-date=29 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240729195237/https://newspaperarchive.com/delphos-herald-jul-02-1990-p-10/ |url-status=live }}</ref> As reported by [[Zoran Sokolović (politician)|Zoran Sokolović]], the president of the Assembly of SR Serbia, in the Official Gazette, the proposal was recommended by the presidency of SR Serbia and members of the Assembly of SR Serbia.<ref name=":6" />

Prior to the announcement, the government had rejected holding elections in 1990.<ref>{{Cite news |date=14 June 1990 |title=Yugoslav protesters demand free elections |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/272545051 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[The Orange County Register]], [[Reuters]] |pages=A28 |id={{ProQuest|272545051}} |archive-date=11 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240711162002/https://www.proquest.com/docview/272545051 |url-status=live }}</ref> The government, however, had discussed with the opposition parties on the implementation of a multi-party system. Nevertheless, the discussion was unsuccessful.<ref name=":88">{{Cite journal |last=Ribić |first=Vladimir |date=2013 |title=Akciona osnova političke mobilizacije srpske opozicije u 1990. godini |trans-title=Action basis of the political mobilisation of the Serbian opposition in 1990 |journal=[[Antropologija]] |language=sr |volume=13 |issue=3}}</ref>{{Rp|page=53}} In response to the announcement, a protest was held at the building of the RTB, criticising the referendum and state controlled media. During the protest, [[Vojislav Šešelj]], the leader of the [[Serbian Chetnik Movement (1990)|Serbian Chetnik Movement]] (SČP), delivered a speech critical of the government.<ref name=":84">{{Cite news |last=Landay |first=Jonathan S. |date=27 June 1990 |title=Hundreds rally against communist control of media |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1990/06/27/Hundreds-rally-against-communist-control-of-media/5724646459200/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[United Press International]]}}</ref> Opposition political parties demanded that the referendum be postponed until after the first multi-party elections, and called on their supporters to [[Election boycott|boycott]] the referendum if the demand was not met.<ref name=":11">{{Cite news |last=Lekić |first=Slobodan |date=5 July 1990 |title=Yugoslavs vote to dissolve legislature |url=https://newspaperarchive.com/new-mexico-gallup-independent-jul-05-1990-p-12/ |access-date=9 July 2024 |work=[[Independent (New Mexico newspaper)|New Mexico Gallup Independent]], [[Associated Press]] |pages=12 |archive-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709195721/https://newspaperarchive.com/new-mexico-gallup-independent-jul-05-1990-p-12/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":19">{{Cite news |date=9 March 2020 |title=Prvi višestranački izbori (1990) |trans-title=First multi-party elections (1990) |url=https://www.b92.net/o/info/izbori2020/vremeplov?yyyy=2020&mm=03&dd=08&nav_id=1664212 |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[B92]] |language=sr |archive-date=10 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710162821/https://www.b92.net/o/info/izbori2020/vremeplov?yyyy=2020&mm=03&dd=08&nav_id=1664212 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The state media labelled those opposing the referendum as "anti-Serbian" and "pro-Albanian".<ref name=":9" /> The results published by the [[Republic Electoral Commission]] (RIK) showed that the referendum passed.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ignjatović |first=Časlav |date=14 July 1990 |title=Izveštaj o rezultatima izjašnjavanja građana u Socijalističkoj Republici Srbiji na republičkom referendumu, održanom 1. i 2. jula 1990. godine, radi potvrđivanja odluke skupštine Socijalističke Republike Srbije da se donese novi ustav SR Srbije |trans-title=Report on the results of the declaration of citizens in the Socialist Republic of Serbia at the republican referendum, held on 1 and 2 July 1990, in order to confirm the decision of the Assembly of the Socialist Republic of Serbia to adopt a new constitution of the SR of Serbia |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6421 |journal=Official Gazette of the Socialist Republic of Serbia |language=sr |volume=35 |issn=0351-5613 |archive-date=9 July 2024 |access-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709195727/http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6421 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 5 July, the government of Serbia dissolved the [[Assembly of the Republic of Kosovo|Assembly of SAP Kosovo]] and its government.<ref name=":11" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=6 July 1990 |title=East bloc in brief |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/244120046 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[Calgary Herald]] |pages=B8 |id={{ProQuest|244120046}} |archive-date=11 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240711162041/https://www.proquest.com/docview/244120046 |url-status=live }}</ref> This was done in response to the proclamation of the [[Republic of Kosova]] three days prior.<ref name=":11" /> The newly adopted constitution effectively abolished SAP Kosovo's autonomy.<ref name=":11" /><ref name=":45">{{Cite news |date=29 September 1990 |title=Serbia shifts power |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/385541149 |access-date=13 July 2024 |work=[[The Globe and Mail]], [[Reuters]] |pages=A8 |id={{ProQuest|385541149}} |archive-date=29 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240729181701/https://www.proquest.com/docview/385541149 |url-status=live }}</ref> "They tried to declare Kosovo a republic and by that they endangered Serbia and Yugoslavia", Sokolović said.<ref>{{Cite news |date=6 July 1990 |title=Ethnic Albanians fired |url=https://newspaperarchive.com/logan-herald-journal-jul-06-1990-p-5/ |access-date=9 July 2024 |work=[[The Herald Journal]], [[Associated Press]] |pages=5 |archive-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709195721/https://newspaperarchive.com/logan-herald-journal-jul-06-1990-p-5/ |url-status=live }}</ref> This move was condemned by [[Amnesty International]] and the [[European Parliament]].<ref name=":5" /> The constitution went into effect on 28 September,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Traynor |first=Ian |date=28 September 1990 |title=Free Hand for Milosevic in Hardline Rule |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/244602374 |access-date=13 July 2024 |work=[[The Guardian]] |pages=12|id={{ProQuest|244602374}} }}</ref> establishing a [[Semi-presidential|semi-presidential system]] in Serbia.<ref name=":61">{{Cite book |last1=Mihailović |first1=Srećko |url=https://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/OKO%20IZBORA%2016.pdf |title=Oko izbora 16: Predsednički izbori 20. januara i 3. februara 2008. godine |last2=Jovanović |first2=Milan |date=April 2008 |publisher=[[CeSID]] |isbn=978-86-83491-46-9 |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=Oko izbora 16: Presidential Elections on 20 January and 3 February 2008 |archive-date=21 June 2024 |access-date=15 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240621024933/https://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/OKO%20IZBORA%2016.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Rp|page=80|pages=}}<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=37|pages=}} Opposition parties criticised the constitution due to the high amount of powers that were given to the president of Serbia.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=80|pages=}}

== Electoral system ==
{{Politics of Serbia}}
With the adoption of a new constitution in September 1990, a new electoral system was introduced in Serbia.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=78}} The 250 members of the National Assembly of Serbia were elected using a [[First-past-the-post voting|first-past-the-post]], [[Two-round system|two-round voting system]] from 250 single-member [[Electoral district|constituencies]].<ref name=":19" /><ref name=":16">{{Cite journal |date=28 September 1990 |title=Zakon o izbornim jedinicama za izbor narodnih poslanika |trans-title=Law on Constituencies for the Election of People's Deputies |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6660 |journal=Official Gazette of the Republic of Serbia |language=sr |volume=1 |issn=0353-8389 |archive-date=9 July 2024 |access-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709225407/http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6660 |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Rp|pages=18–20}}<ref name=":110">{{Cite book |last=Orlović |first=Slaviša |title=Partije i izbori u Srbiji: 20 godina |publisher=[[Friedrich Ebert Foundation]], [[Faculty of Political Sciences, University of Belgrade|Faculty of Political Sciences]], Centre for Democracy |year=2011 |isbn=978-86-84031-49-7 |edition=1 |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=Parties and elections in Serbia: 20 years |oclc=785849369}}</ref>{{Rp|page=233}} All subsequent elections in Serbia have been conducted under a [[proportional representation system]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=24 April 2016 |title=Kako smo glasali od 1990. do 2014. |trans-title=How we voted from 1990 to 2014 |url=https://www.rts.rs/lat/vesti/izbori-2016/2294636/kako-smo-glasali-od-1990-do-2014-.html |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[Radio Television of Serbia]] |language=sr |archive-date=10 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710162821/https://www.rts.rs/lat/vesti/izbori-2016/2294636/kako-smo-glasali-od-1990-do-2014-.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":33">{{Cite news |last=Sejdinović |first=Nedim |date=17 November 2021 |title=Sa njima nema neizvesnosti |trans-title=There is no uncertainty with them |url=https://vreme.com/vreme/sa-njima-nema-neizvesnosti/ |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[Vreme]] |language=sr |archive-date=10 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710214507/https://vreme.com/vreme/sa-njima-nema-neizvesnosti/ |url-status=live }}</ref> A constituency's territory could consist of multiple [[Local community (Bosnia and Herzegovina)|local communities]] (''mesna zajednica''), populated places (''naseljena mesta''), or a single municipality.<ref name=":16" />{{Rp|pages=20–29}} Eligible voters were able to vote for only one candidate in their respective constituencies. A campaign for an election could have lasted 30 to 90 days.<ref name=":17" /> 

A candidate could be nominated by one or more political parties or other registered political organisations with at least 100 valid signatures from constituency residents, or by a {{Ill|citizens group|lt=citizens group|sr|Група грађана}} (''grupa građana'') instead. A citizens group, under the law enacted in 1990, is a group of 100 citizens bound by an agreement willing to take part in an election. The candidate was submitted to RIK by its proposer. RIK could deny a candidate, after which the deficiencies had to be corrected within 48 hours if the candidate was to be re-submitted again. The symbol of a constituency, the name of the election, and a list of candidates that ran in the constituency were present on the voting ballot.<ref name=":17">{{Cite journal |date=28 September 1990 |title=Zakon o izbornu narodnih poslanika |trans-title=Law on the Election of People's Deputies |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6659 |journal=Official Gazette of the Republic of Serbia |language=sr |volume=1 |pages=13–18 |issn=0353-8389 |archive-date=9 July 2024 |access-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709225405/http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6659 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the first round, a candidate was elected if it earned at least 50% of the popular vote, while in the second round the candidate with the most votes won.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Novaković |first=G. |date=22 January 2009 |title=Od 250 do jedne izborne jedinice |trans-title=From 250 to one electoral constituency |url=https://www.politika.rs/sr/clanak/72133/Od-250-do-jedne-izborne-jedinice |access-date=16 July 2024 |work=[[Politika]] |language=sr |archive-date=24 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241224160315/https://www.politika.rs/sr/clanak/72133/Od-250-do-jedne-izborne-jedinice |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the presidential election, a candidate had to be a resident of Serbia that was proposed by one or more political parties, political organisations, or a citizens group. A candidate was elected if it received a majority of all votes cast and if at least half of all voters participated in the election. If no candidate received a majority of all votes cast, the second round had to be held within the next fifteen days. In the second round, at least two of the candidates with the highest votes compete.<ref name=":18">{{Cite journal |date=28 September 1990 |title=Zakon o izboru predsednika republike |trans-title=The Law on the Election of the President of the Republic |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6658 |journal=Official Gazette of the Republic of Serbia |language=sr |volume=1 |issue=12 |issn=0353-8389 |archive-date=9 July 2024 |access-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709225409/http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6658 |url-status=live }}</ref> An election was considered valid if the [[Voter turnout|turnout]] was at least 50%; otherwise, a [[snap election]] is scheduled.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=82|pages=}}<ref name=":18" /> The newly established position of the president of Serbia was not ceremonial, instead it had significant powers and served as part of the executive branch of the government.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=|pages=79–80}}<ref name=":62">{{Cite book |last=Goati |first=Vladimir |url=https://www.cesid.rs/images/IZBORI%20U%20SRJ%20OD%201990_%20DO%201998.pdf |title=Izbori u SRJ: od 1990. do 1998.: volja građana ili izborna manipulacija; Dodatak: Izbori 2000 |date=2001 |publisher=[[CeSID]] |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=Elections in the FRY: from 1990 to 1998: the will of the citizens or electoral manipulation; Appendix: 2000 elections}}</ref>{{Rp|page=37|pages=}}

RIK, local election commissions, and polling boards of constituencies have overseen elections in Serbia.<ref name=":17" /><ref>{{Cite journal |date=22 November 1990 |title=Odluka o ovlašćenju izbornih komisija i biračkih odbora u izbornim jedinicama za vršenje određenih radnji u sprovođenju izbora za predsednika republike |trans-title=Decision on the authorisation of election commissions and polling boards in electoral units to perform certain actions in conducting elections for the president of the republic |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6766 |journal=Official Gazette of the Republic of Serbia |language=sr |volume=11 |pages=325 |issn=0353-8389 |archive-date=9 July 2024 |access-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709225408/http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6766 |url-status=live }}</ref> At the time of the election, [[Časlav Ignjatović]] served as the president of RIK.<ref name=":14">{{Cite journal |last=Sokolović |first=Zoran |date=28 September 1990 |title=Odluka o raspisivanju izbora za predsednika Republike Srbije, odluka o raspisivanju izbora za narodne poslanike u Narodnu skupštinu Republike Srbije i odluka o imenovanju predsednika, sekretara, članova i zamenika članova Republičke izborne komisije |trans-title=Decision on calling for elections for the President of the Republic of Serbia, decision on calling for elections for deputies to the National Assembly of the Republic of Serbia and decision on appointing the president, secretary, members and deputy members of the Republic Electoral Commission |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6662 |journal=Official Gazette of the Republic of Serbia |language=sr |volume=1 |pages=30 |issn=0353-8389}}</ref> Parliamentary and presidential elections were called by the president of the National Assembly, who also had to announce their dates.<ref name=":17" /><ref name=":18" /> Regarding the 1990 election, the presidential and parliamentary elections were called by Sokolović on 28 September for 9 December.<ref name=":19" /><ref name=":14" /> In the event of run-offs, the second round of the parliamentary election was set for 23 December.<ref name=":48">{{Cite news |date=9 December 1990 |title=On the Ballot in Yugoslavia |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/108438954 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[New York Times]] |pages=22|id={{ProQuest|108438954}} }}</ref> According to the law, a parliamentary election is held every four years, though it is possible for a snap election to take place. If a snap election occurs, then the president of Serbia has to call the election, as well as dissolve the National Assembly.<ref name=":17" /> A presidential election is held every five years.<ref name=":48" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=2 March 2017 |title=Svi predsednički izbori u Srbiji |trans-title=All presidential elections in Serbia |url=https://www.rts.rs/lat/vesti/izbori-2017/2649972/svi-predsednicki-izbori-u-srbiji-.html |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[Radio Television of Serbia]] |language=sr}}</ref>

To vote, a person had to be a citizen, able to perform working duties, and at least 18 years old.<ref name=":17" /> Those in the military had the right to vote at military establishments.<ref name=":17" /><ref name=":24">{{Cite news |last=Bohlen |first=Celestine |date=10 December 1990 |title=Ex-Communist Chief Takes The Lead in Serbia's Election |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/12/10/world/ex-communist-chief-takes-the-lead-in-serbia-s-election.html |access-date=22 August 2023 |work=[[The New York Times]] |page=A6 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=5 September 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170905005735/https://www.nytimes.com/1990/12/10/world/ex-communist-chief-takes-the-lead-in-serbia-s-election.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Voting also took place in hospitals, nursing homes, and police barracks.<ref name=":24" /> During the election day, eligible voters could have voted from 07:00 ([[UTC+01:00]]) to 20:00 at a voting station in their constituency. Voters who were either blind, disabled, or illiterate could have brought a relative to vote on their behalf at a voting station.<ref name=":17" /> This was the first multi-party election in Serbia.<ref name=":45" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=8 March 2017 |title=Kako se glasalo na predsedničkim izborima |trans-title=How did we vote in the presidential elections |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/kako-se-glasalo-na-predsednickim-izborima/ |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr |archive-date=10 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710190332/https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/kako-se-glasalo-na-predsednickim-izborima/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Signatures dispute ===
Shortly before the adoption of the new constitution and the news laws, both proposed election laws, the Law on Constituencies for the Election of People's Deputies and the Law on the Election of the President of the Republic, stated that a much larger number of collected signatures was required to submit candidates; for the parliamentary election, the number was 500, while for the presidential election, the number was 10,000.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=81|pages=}} This was strongly opposed by opposition parties; they claimed that this would effectively bar them from participating in the elections.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=81|pages=}}<ref name=":29">{{Cite book |last=Thomas |first=Robert |title=Serbia under Milošević: Politics in the 1990s |publisher=[[C. Hurst & Co.]] |year=1998 |isbn=978-1-85065-367-7 |edition=1 |location=London}}</ref>{{Rp|pages=|page=71}} The opposition wanted the number of collected signatures that were required to participate in the elections to be lowered.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=81|pages=}} For the presidential elections, they proposed the number to be 50.<ref name=":88" />{{Rp|page=55}}

The government accepted the opposition's proposal; however, while they lowered the number of needed signatures for the parliamentary elections to 100, they also lowered the number of needed signatures for presidential elections to 100.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=81|pages=}}<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=72}} As a result, 34 candidates submitted for the presidency; 32 candidates were ultimately accepted to participate in the presidential elections.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=82|pages=}}<ref name=":63" />

=== Political parties ===
{{See also|List of political parties in Serbia}}
The table below lists political parties elected to the Assembly of SR Serbia after the 1989 parliamentary election. At the time of the 1989 election, [[Bogdan Trifunović]] was the president of the presidency of the Central Committee of SKS.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Da Graça |first=John |title=Heads of State and Government |publisher=[[Palgrave Macmillan]] |year=2017 |isbn=978-1-349-65771-1 |location=London |pages=1094}}</ref> During its existence, the Assembly of SR Serbia was divided into the Council of Associated Labour, the Council of Municipalities, and the Socio-Political Council. In the Council of Associated Labour, 134 SKS delegates were elected; in the Council of Municipalities, 84 SKS delegates were elected; and in the Socio-Political Council, 85 SKS delegates were elected.<ref name=":38">{{Cite journal |date=December 1990 |title=Statistički godišnjak SR Srbije 1990. |trans-title=Statistical Yearbook of SR Serbia in 1990 |journal=Statistički godišnjak SR Srbije |language=sr |location=Belgrade |publisher=Republic Bureau of Statistics |issn=0351-4064}}</ref>{{Rp|page=29}}

