Although there was dissent during the one-party period, the formation of other political parties was prohibited. By the time of the [[Revolutions of 1989]], other republics adopted [[Pluralism (political theory)|political pluralism]], forcing the SKS to relax its positions, which led to the emergence of opposition parties.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=32, 52}} The registry of political parties was established with the passage of the Law on Political Organisations in July 1990.<ref name=":13">{{Cite journal |last=Vladisavljević |first=Sreten |date=27 September 1990 |title=Rešenje o upisu političkih organizacija u registar političkih organizacija |trans-title=Decision on registration of political organisations in the register of political organisations |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6669 |journal=Official Gazette of the Socialist Republic of Serbia |language=sr |volume=55 |pages=2266–2267 |issn=0351-5613 |archive-date=9 July 2024 |access-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709195724/http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6669 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":67">{{Cite journal |last=Sotirović |first=Vladislav B. |date=2010 |title=The Multiparty Elections in Serbia in 1990 |journal=[[Political Science Almanac|Politikos mokslų almanachas]] |volume=1 |issue=6 |issn=2029-0225}}</ref>{{Rp|pages=|page=10}} The first political parties in the registry included the [[Socialist Party of Serbia]] (SPS), the legal successor of SKS,<ref name=":19" /> Serbian Renewal Movement (SPO), [[Serbian National Renewal]] (SNO), [[Serbian Saint Sava Party]], People's Radical Party (NRS), Democratic Party (DS), [[New Democracy – Movement for Serbia]] (ND), [[Democratic Fellowship of Vojvodina Hungarians]], [[Party of Independent Entrepreneurs and Peasants]], [[New Communist Party of Yugoslavia|New Communist Movement]], [[Workers' Party of Yugoslavia]], [[Democratic Forum (Serbia)|Democratic Forum]], [[Party of Democratic Action of Sandžak|Party of Democratic Action]] (SDA), [[Movement for the Protection of Human Rights]], [[Alliance of All Serbs of the World]], [[Democratic Alliance of Croats in Vojvodina]], [[Independent Democratic Association]], [[Party for Democratic Action]], [[Republican Party (Serbia, 1990)|Republican Party]], [[Old Radical Party]], [[People's Party (Serbia, 1990)|People's Party]] (NS), [[Green Party (Serbia, 1990)|Green Party]] (ZS), [[Democratic Party of Freedom]], Liberal Party (LS), [[Democratic Party (Davidović–Grol)]], [[Democratic Political Party Roma]], [[Party of Social Justice (Serbia)|Party of Social Justice]], [[People's Peasant Party]] (NSS), [[Serb Democratic Party (Serbia, 1990–2003)|Serb Democratic Party]], and [[Party of Yugoslavs]].<ref name=":13" />

Given that SPS was formed as a merger of SKS and the [[Socialist Alliance of Working People of Yugoslavia]], it inherited a significant amount of political infrastructure, as well as material and financial assets.<ref name=":19" /><ref>{{Cite book |last=Wiatr |first=Jerzy J. |url= |title=Europa pokomunistyczna przemiany państw i społeczeństw po 1989 roku |publisher=Wydawnictwo Naukowe Scholar |year=2006 |isbn=978-83-7383-207-7 |location=Warsaw |pages=178 |language=pl |trans-title=Post-communist Europe, transformations of states and societies after 1989 |access-date=}}</ref><ref name=":31">{{Cite news |last=Miladinović |first=Aleksandar |date=9 December 2020 |title=Prvi moderni višestranački izbori u Srbiji: Šta je odlučilo prvi "praznik demokratije" |trans-title=The first modern multi-party elections in Serbia: What decided the first "holiday of democracy" |url=https://www.bbc.com/serbian/lat/srbija-55240178 |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=sr |archive-date=5 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230105142419/https://www.bbc.com/serbian/lat/srbija-55240178 |url-status=live }}</ref> With these resources and its continued influence over major industries, SPS was able to maintain dominance over political events in Serbia.<ref name=":19" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Harden |first=Blaine |date=20 September 1990 |title=Nationalist Rivalry Slows Yugoslavia's Recovery;Next Step in Economic Reform Controversial |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/307307518 |access-date=13 July 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |pages=A40 |id={{ProQuest|307307518}}}}</ref> Milošević was the president of SPS.<ref>{{Cite news |date=18 July 1990 |title=Serbian populist leads new party Elections, market economy part of platform |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/436248122 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[Toronto Star]], [[Reuters]] |pages=A15 |id={{ProQuest|436248122}} |archive-date=11 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240711163505/https://www.proquest.com/docview/436248122 |url-status=live }}</ref> DS was founded in December 1989, when a group of intellectuals announced the revival of the interwar [[Democratic Party (Yugoslavia)|Democratic Party]].<ref name=":30">{{Cite web |last=Kojić |first=Nikola |date=10 February 2020 |title=Izbori 1990: Rekordna izlaznost, glumački okršaj i istorijska pobeda socijalista |trans-title=1990 elections: Record turnout, clashes and historic victory of the Socialists |url=https://n1info.rs/vesti/a568114-izbori-1990-rekordna-izlaznost-glumacki-okrsaj-i-istorijska-pobeda-socijalista/ |access-date=10 July 2024 |website=[[N1 (TV channel)|N1]] |language=sr |archive-date=29 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240729193728/https://n1info.rs/vesti/a568114-izbori-1990-rekordna-izlaznost-glumacki-okrsaj-i-istorijska-pobeda-socijalista/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Mataric-2006">{{Cite journal |last1=Matarić |first1=Maja J. |last2=Ninković |first2=Višnja |date=2006 |title=Remembering the Beginnings of the (re-established) Democratic Party |url= |journal=The South Slav Journal |publisher=Dositej Obradović Circle |publication-place=London |volume=27 |issue=3–4 |pages=62–71 |issn=0141-6146 }}</ref> Dragoljub Mićunović and [[Kosta Čavoški]] were the candidates in the inaugural DS leadership election.<ref name="Stojiljkovic-2013">{{Cite book |last1=Stojiljković |first1=Zoran |title=(Ne)demokratsko redizajniranje političkih partija Srbije |last2=Pilipović |first2=Gordana |last3=Spasojević |first3=Dušan |date=2013 |publisher=[[Konrad Adenauer Foundation]] |isbn=978-86-86661-69-2 |location=Belgrade |pages=27 |language=sr |trans-title=The (Un)Democratic Redesign of Serbia's Political Parties}}</ref> Mićunović and Čavoški held opposing political beliefs, with Mićunović being a liberal and Čavoški being an anti-communist nationalist.<ref name="Stojiljkovic-2013" /><ref name="Vukadinovic-20022">{{Cite web |last=Vukadinović |first=Đorđe |date=16 January 2002 |title=Čovek na mestu ili konac delo krasi |trans-title=A man in a place or a thread adorns the work |url=https://www.vreme.com/vreme/covek-na-mestu-ili-konac-delo-krasi/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121124311/https://www.vreme.com/vreme/covek-na-mestu-ili-konac-delo-krasi/ |archive-date=21 January 2023 |access-date=21 January 2023 |website=[[Vreme]] |language=sr }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Zlojutro |first=Darko |date=12 December 2019 |title=Njih 13 obnovilo je rad Demokratske stranke i sebi potpisalo smrtnu presudu! Mnogi su pobegli iz DS u druge tabore |trans-title=13 of them renewed the Democratic Party and signed themselves a death sentence! Many fled from DS to other political camps |url=https://www.espreso.co.rs/vesti/politika/480367/njih-13-obnovilo-je-rad-demokratske-stranke-i-sebi-potpisali-smrtnu-presudu-mnogi-su-pobegli-iz-ds-u-druge-tabore |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230120192535/https://www.espreso.co.rs/vesti/politika/480367/njih-13-obnovilo-je-rad-demokratske-stranke-i-sebi-potpisali-smrtnu-presudu-mnogi-su-pobegli-iz-ds-u-druge-tabore |archive-date=20 January 2023 |access-date=20 January 2023 |website=Espreso |language=sr }}</ref> Mićunović won the leadership election and led DS in the 1990 parliamentary election.<ref name="Stojiljkovic-2013" />

[[Vuk Drašković]], а prominent writer known for his nationalist books in the 1980s, was the co-founder of SPO.<ref name=":110" />{{Rp|page=143}}<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=39}} Drašković was first affiliated with the Saint Sava Association before joining the Serbian National Renewal (SNO).<ref name=":42">{{Cite book |last=Bugajski |first=Janusz |author-link=Janusz Bugajski |title=Political parties of Eastern Europe: A Guide to Politics in the Post-Communist Era |publisher=[[Routledge]] |year=2019 |isbn=978-1-000-16135-9 |edition=1 |location=London, United Kingdom |oclc=1197811512}}</ref>{{Rp|pages=581–582}} In March 1990, SNO president [[Mirko Jović]] verbally attacked Drašković for his prior membership in SKJ, causing a schism within the party.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=53}} Drašković then left SNO and formed SPO with Šešelj.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=54}}<ref name=":30" /> Drašković was also elected its president.<ref name=":30" /> SPO was an [[extreme nationalist]] party during the 1990 elections, with Drašković described as a "serious threat" to Milošević.<ref name=":35">{{Cite news |last=Williams |first=Carol J. |date=14 June 1990 |title=Soldiers Fire on Protesters as Romania Erupts Again |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/281163843 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |pages=1 |id={{ProQuest|281163843}} |archive-date=29 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240729193747/https://www.proquest.com/docview/281163843 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Silber |first=Laura |date=11 November 1990 |title=Elections may alter Yugoslavia's delicate balance |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/407101182 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |pages=22A |id={{ProQuest|407101182}}}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=14 June 1990 |title=Yugoslav Protesters Demand Serbia Vote |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/302545748 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[San Francisco Chronicle]] |pages=A27 |id={{ProQuest|302545748}} |archive-date=11 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240711162041/https://www.proquest.com/docview/302545748 |url-status=live }}</ref> Šešelj left SPO in May 1990 after disagreements about boycotting [[Siniša Kovačević]]'s play ''Saint Sava''.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=55}}<ref name=":30" /> He then formed SČP in June 1990, although it remained an unregistered party.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=ix}}<ref name=":34">{{Cite news |last=Čongradin |first=Snežana |date=9 December 2022 |title=Kako je Milošević potukao SPO i DS očiglednom medijskom kontrolom: Pre 32 godine održani prvi višestranački izbori u Srbiji |trans-title=How Milošević beat SPO and DS with obvious media control: The first multi-party elections in Serbia were held 32 years ago |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/kako-je-milosevic-potukao-spo-i-ds-ociglednom-medijskom-kontrolom-pre-32-godine-odrzani-prvi-visestranacki-izbori-u-srbiji/ |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[Danas (newspaper)|Danas]] |language=sr |archive-date=10 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710214507/https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/kako-je-milosevic-potukao-spo-i-ds-ociglednom-medijskom-kontrolom-pre-32-godine-odrzani-prvi-visestranacki-izbori-u-srbiji/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In 1991, SČP became the [[Serbian Radical Party]] (SRS).<ref name=":30" />

== Participants ==
=== Parliamentary candidates ===
