The parliamentary election was contested by 1,701 candidates,<ref name=":202">{{Cite book |last=Stokić |first=Ljubiša B. |title=Demokratija i osvajanje vlasti: izbori u Srbiji 1990. |publisher=[[Zenit (publisher)|Zenit]] |year=1994 |isbn=978-86-81987-06-3 |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=Democracy and the conquest of power: the elections in Serbia in 1990}}</ref>{{Rp|page=604}} 81 of whom were women,<ref name=":30" /> who were either proposed by a registered political party or citizens groups.<ref name=":17" /><ref name=":25">{{Cite news |date=29 April 2008 |title=Izbori 1990 |trans-title=1990 elections |url=https://vreme.com/vreme/izbori-1990/ |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[Vreme]] |language=sr |archive-date=10 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710190333/https://vreme.com/vreme/izbori-1990/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Out of that number, 342 candidates were proposed by a citizens group.<ref name=":202" />{{Rp|page=604}} The second round of the parliamentary election was contested by 303 candidates.<ref name=":25" /> At the time of the election, there were 56 registered parties.<ref name=":89">{{Cite thesis |last=Matić |first=Jovanka |title=Televizijska prezentacija izbornih kampanja za parlamentarne izbore u Srbiji 1990–2000 [Television Presentation of Election Campaigns for Parliamentary Elections in Serbia 1990–2000] |date=2006 |publisher=[[University of Belgrade]] |doi=10.2298/BG20060717MATIC |language=sr |place=Belgrade |chapter=1990: Prvi višestranački parlamentarni izbori [1990: First multi-party parliamentary elections]|hdl=21.15107/rcub_nardus_3081 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>{{Rp|page=47}} The three main political parties in Serbia at the time of the election were SPS, SPO, and DS.<ref name=":30" />

In the election, SPS nominated 250 candidates, followed by SPO which nominated 237 candidates, and DS which nominated 179 candidates.<ref name=":202" />{{Rp|page=606}} The elections were also contested by a large number of public figures.<ref name=":30" /> SPS candidates [[Bata Živojinović]], [[Mihailo Janketić]], and [[Miroslav Ilić]], [[Union of Reform Forces of Yugoslavia|Union of Reform Forces of Yugoslavia in Serbia]] (SRSJS) candidates [[Bora Todorović]] and [[Ljuba Tadić]], and DS candidates [[Svetislav Basara]] and [[Gojko Đogo]] competed in the election.<ref name=":30" /> [[Vojislav Brajović]], [[Ljubivoje Ršumović]], [[Branko Milićević]], and [[Minja Subota]] also contested the elections on behalf of citizens groups.<ref name=":30" /><ref name=":74" />

[[Kosovo Albanians]], 700,000 of whom were registered to vote in the 1990 elections, boycotted the election.<ref name=":33" /><ref name=":22">{{Cite news |date=9 December 2020 |title=Trideset godina od prvih višestranačkih i predsedničkih izbora u Srbiji |trans-title=Thirty years since the first multi-party and presidential elections in Serbia |url=https://www.politika.rs/sr/clanak/468442/Trideset-godina-od-prvih-visestranackih-i-predsednickih-izbora-u-Srbiji |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[Politika]] |language=sr |archive-date=29 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240729182207/https://www.politika.rs/sr/clanak/468442/Trideset-godina-od-prvih-visestranackih-i-predsednickih-izbora-u-Srbiji |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Adams |first1=Noah |last2=Harden |first2=Blaine |date=10 December 1990 |title=Yugoslavia Elects Communist as President |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/190007722 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[NPR]], [[The Washington Post]] |pages=1 |id={{ProQuest|190007722}} |archive-date=11 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240711175051/https://www.proquest.com/docview/190007722 |url-status=live }}</ref> As a result, SPS was able to sweep the constituencies in Kosovo, winning five without opposition.<ref name=":33" /><ref name=":30" /> Before the election campaign began, Kosovo Albanians staged protests and strikes, which were ultimately suppressed by the government of Serbia.<ref>{{Cite news |date=30 August 1990 |title=Albanian Protest Broken Up in Yugoslavia |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/108508703 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]] |pages=A8|id={{ProQuest|108508703}} }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Wertheimer |first=Linda |date=3 September 1990 |title=Ethnic Albanians Strike in Yugoslavia |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/189805503 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[NPR]] |pages=1 |id={{ProQuest|189805503}} |archive-date=11 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240711163505/https://www.proquest.com/docview/189805503 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Sudetic |first=Chuck |date=4 September 1990 |title=Strike Paralyzes Yugoslav Region: Ethnic Albanians in Kosovo Protest the Takeover by Serbia and Dismissals |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/108500883 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]] |pages=A6|id={{ProQuest|108500883}} }}</ref> According to ''[[Reuters]]'', opposition parties in Kosovo also said that "they would not respect Serbia's new constitution".<ref>{{Cite news |date=29 September 1990 |title=Ethnic strife has Yugoslavia on knife-edge; Ethnic clashes threaten to tear Yugoslavia apart |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/243477284 |access-date=13 July 2024 |work=[[The Vancouver Sun]], [[Reuters]] |pages=A1|id={{ProQuest|243477284}} }}</ref> [[Ibrahim Rugova]], the president of the [[Democratic League of Kosovo]] (LDK), said that "to participate in these elections would mean that we accept the conditions the Serbians have imposed upon us".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Harden |first=Blaine |date=10 December 1990 |title=Ethnic Albanians Boycott Key Election in Yugoslav Province |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/307315984 |access-date=14 July 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |pages=A20 |id={{ProQuest|307315984}} |archive-date=29 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240729182207/https://www.proquest.com/docview/307315984 |url-status=live }}</ref> Kosovo Albanians continued to boycott the elections until 1997.<ref name=":59">{{Cite book |last1=Nedović |first1=Slobodanka |url=http://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/OKO%20IZBORA%201.pdf |title=Oko izbora 1: Izveštaj Centra za slobodne izbore i demokratiju sa parlamentarnih i predsedničkih izbora u Srbiji (septembar-oktobar 1997) |last2=Goati |first2=Vladimir |publisher=[[CeSID]] |year=1997 |edition=1 |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=Oko izbora 1: Report of the Centre for Free Elections and Democracy on parliamentary and presidential elections in Serbia (September–October 1997) |oclc=491063176 |archive-date=19 January 2019 |access-date=17 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190119121002/http://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/OKO%20IZBORA%201.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Rp|page=12}}

=== Presidential candidates ===
The following list includes candidates who took part in the presidential election.<ref>{{Cite journal |date=2 December 1990 |title=Lista kandidata za izbor predsednika republike 9. decembra 1990. godine |trans-title=List of candidates for the election of the President of the Republic on 9 December 1990 |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6826 |journal=Official Gazette of the Republic of Serbia |language=sr |volume=15 |pages=458 |issn=0353-8389 |archive-date=9 July 2024 |access-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709225412/http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6826 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |date=7 December 1990 |title=Ispravka liste kandidata za izbor predsednika republike 9. decembra 1990. godine |trans-title=Correction of the list of candidates for the election of the President of the Republic on 9 December 1990 |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=103766 |journal=Official Gazette of the Republic of Serbia |language=sr |volume=17 |pages=529 |issn=0353-8389 |archive-date=9 July 2024 |access-date=9 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709225818/http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=103766 |url-status=live }}</ref> The election had 32 contestants, which has remained the highest number of candidates in any presidential election in Serbia since then.<ref name=":63">{{Cite news |date=20 March 2017 |title=Predsednički izbori 1990. godine |trans-title=1990 presidential elections |url=https://vreme.com/vreme/predsednicki-izbori-1990-godine/ |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[Vreme]] |language=sr |archive-date=10 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710202257/https://vreme.com/vreme/predsednicki-izbori-1990-godine/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Andrić |first=Gordana |date=11 April 2012 |title=Serbian Presidential Elections Since 1990 |url=https://balkaninsight.com/2012/04/11/serbian-presidential-elections-since-1990/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[Balkan Insight]] |archive-date=20 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240720132612/https://balkaninsight.com/2012/04/11/serbian-presidential-elections-since-1990/ |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Ljiljana Ćuić]] became the first female to contest a presidential election.<ref name=":22" />
