The election campaign began on 28 September, when the president of the National Assembly called for the elections to be held.<ref name=":14" /> According to Bojan Klačar of the [[Centre for Free Elections and Democracy]] (CeSID), "SPS entered the elections with such a large infrastructure inherited from the League of Communists that it could not have serious competition" (''SPS je ušao u izbore sa toliko velikom infrastrukturom nasleđenom od Saveza komunista da nije mogao imati ozbiljnu konkurenciju'').<ref name=":31" /> The infrastructure that SPS gained after its formation was worth approximately [[US$]]160 million,<ref name=":88" />{{Rp|page=54}}<ref name=":30" /> giving SPS an immeasurable advantage.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=83|pages=}}<ref name=":89" />{{Rp|page=48}} The fairness of the election was questioned by opposition parties since the campaign began.<ref name=":89" />{{Rp|page=39}} Anti-government protests, mainly orchestrated by SPO and DS under the umbrella of the [[United Opposition of Serbia (1990)|United Opposition of Serbia]] (UOS), were held during the campaign period,<ref name=":67" />{{Rp|pages=|page=13}}<ref name=":26">{{Cite news |last=Drčelić |first=Zora |date=14 March 2012 |title=Kanabe nas je održalo |trans-title=Kanabe kept us going |url=https://vreme.com/vreme/kanabe-nas-je-odrzalo/ |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[Vreme]] |language=sr |archive-date=10 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710190331/https://vreme.com/vreme/kanabe-nas-je-odrzalo/ |url-status=live }}</ref> protesting against unfair treatment of the opposition on the RTB.<ref name=":26" />

Milošević and Drašković were the leading presidential candidates during the campaign period, with Milošević considered to be the favourite.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=82|pages=}}<ref name=":48" /><ref name=":49">{{Cite news |date=9 December 1990 |title=Relative unknown challenges Walesa in battle for presidency Series: ELECTION ROUNDUP |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/262784168 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[St. Petersburg Times]] |pages=A23|id={{ProQuest|262784168}} }}</ref> SPS and its precursor had already adopted nationalist rhetoric when Milošević came to power in 1987.<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=|pages=30}}<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Dúró |first1=József |title=Political History of Balkans (1989–2018) |last2=Egeresi |first2=Zoltán |date=2020 |publisher=Dialog Campus |isbn=978-963-531-162-0 |location=Budapest |pages=142}}</ref> ''[[NIN (magazine)|NIN]]'' described the campaign as taking place under "extraordinarily dramatic circumstances" (''izvanredno dramatičnim okolnostima'').<ref name=":82">{{Cite news |date=20 September 2023 |title=Decenija krađa i beznađa |trans-title=A decade of theft and hopelessness |url=https://www.nin.rs/arhiva/vesti/36318/decenija-krada-i-beznada |access-date=19 July 2024 |work=[[NIN (magazine)|NIN]] |language=sr}}</ref> ''[[The New York Times]]'' called the election conclusive because of Serbia's role within Yugoslavia, while the ''[[Los Angeles Times]]'' stated that "the election also provides a last chance for Yugoslavia to reconsider its headlong rush toward disintegration and civil war".<ref name=":39">{{Cite news |last=Bohlen |first=Celestine |date=9 December 1990 |title=Serbian Voting Today Could Signal A Major Turn in Yugoslavia's Future |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/108439731 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]] |pages=22|id={{ProQuest|108439731}} }}</ref><ref name=":47">{{Cite news |last=Williams |first=Carol J. |date=8 December 1990 |title=Nationalism Yugoslav Unity on Line in Serbian Vote It's seen as a last chance to avert a rush toward disintegration. But the forces of peace are trailing |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/281206835 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |pages=3|id={{ProQuest|281206835}} }}</ref> According to the ''[[Toronto Star]]'', the elections "may determine, to a large extent, the future make-up or breakup of Yugoslavia", while ''[[The Washington Post]]'' said that "the survival of Yugoslavia may well be at stake" in the Serbian elections.<ref name=":50">{{Cite news |last=Doder |first=Duško |date=9 December 1990 |title=Serbs threaten breakup of Yugoslavia |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/436315719 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Toronto Star]] |pages=H2 |id={{ProQuest|436315719}} |archive-date=29 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240729182717/https://www.proquest.com/docview/436315719 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":51">{{Cite news |last=Harden |first=Blaine |date=9 December 1990 |title=Elections Today May Determine Yugoslavia's Fate;Two Top Candidates in Serbia Focus on Virulent Nationalism |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/307331582 |access-date=14 July 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |pages=A29|id={{ProQuest|307331582}} }}</ref> Shortly before the election, the ''[[San Francisco Chronicle]]'' predicted that Serbia "is expected to remain staunchly Communist" after the elections.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=McAdams |first1=Michael |last2=Urban |first2=Željko |last3=Urb |first3=Tomislav |date=4 December 1990 |title=Drive for Self-Determination May Break Up Yugoslavia |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/302608846 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[San Francisco Chronicle]] |pages=A21|id={{ProQuest|302608846}} }}</ref> ''[[The Seattle Times]]'' considered the presidential election to be the most important one that year in Yugoslavia.<ref>{{Cite news |date=9 December 1990 |title=News in brief |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/384456896 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[The Seattle Times]] |pages=A2|id={{ProQuest|384456896}} }}</ref> By the end of the campaign, the Serbian opposition, Croatia, Slovenia, and Western countries viewed Drašković as a more acceptable option as president than Milošević.<ref name=":51" />

Throughout the campaign, opposition candidates lacked funding and infrastructure.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=83|pages=}}<ref name=":67" />{{Rp|pages=|page=11}}<ref name=":50" /> Most opposition parties lacked skilled or motivated members, activists, financial resources, and other infrastructure required to run an effective campaign.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=83|pages=}} Publicist [[Zlatoje Martinov]] claimed that the campaign was unfair because the media heavily backed SPS while harshly criticising opposition politicians.<ref name="Martinov2000" />{{Rp|page=24}}

Both SPO and DS claimed to have tight relations with the [[Serb Democratic Party (Bosnia and Herzegovina)|Serb Democratic Party]] (SDS) of [[Radovan Karadžić]] during the campaign.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=56}}<ref>{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=9 December 2010 |title=Početak višestranačja na četiri M |trans-title=The beginning of multi-partyism on four M's |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/pocetak-visestranacja-na-cetiri-m/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr |archive-date=14 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230814115026/https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/pocetak-visestranacja-na-cetiri-m/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The {{Ill|Veterans' Union of Yugoslavia|lt=Veterans' Union|sr|Савез удружења бораца Народноослободилачког рата Југославије}} also expressed its support for SPS, stating that "SPS is the only party that can oppose the right-wing and nationalist elements and mediaeval darkness, Albanian separatism, and [[Vojvodina autonomism]]" (''SPS je jedina stranka koja se može susprotstaviti desničarskoj i nacionalističkoj stihiji i srednjovekovnom mraku, albanskom separatizmu i vojvođanskom autonomaštvu'').<ref name=":74">{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=21 December 2020 |title=I Branko Kockica u trci za poslaničko mesto |trans-title=And Branko Kockica is in the race for the parliamentary seat |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/i-branko-kockica-u-trci-za-poslanicko-mesto/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr}}</ref>

=== Slogans ===
The table below lists slogans that political parties employed during the campaign.<ref name=":31" /> [[Nikola Šainović]], an SPS official and future prime minister of Serbia, stated that "we believed in that slogan" (''mi smo u tu parolu verovali'') and that "it was not just a marketing slogan, there was practically no marketing then, it was a real political slogan" (''to nije bila samo marketinška parola, tada marketnga praktično nije ni bilo, to je bila prava politička parola'').<ref name=":31" /> The SPS slogan "there is no uncertainty with us" (''sa nama nema neizvesnosti'') was also featured on their election posters.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Atlagić |first=Siniša |date=2018 |title=Politička slika sveta na izbornom plakatu u Srbiji od 1990. do 2017. godine |trans-title=The political picture of the world on the election poster in Serbia from 1990 to 2017 |journal=[[Communication and Media]] |language=sr |volume=13 |issue=13 |pages=36 |issn=2466-541X}}</ref> Mićunović said that "that slogan spoke of our desire to say that we were wrong a lot, so it's time to decide wisely" (''ta parola je govorila o našoj želji da kažemo da smo dosta grešili, pa je vreme da prelomimo pametno'').<ref name=":31" /> SPO used slogan acronyms, with the first letters of the words in Serbian spelling out their abbreviations.<ref name=":30" />

