=== Media promotion ===
Klačar argued that political communication was free during the campaign period; political scientist Jovanka Matić, in CeSID's ''Mediji i izbori'' (Media and Elections) book, stated that there was no equal treatment of election participants and that the 1990 elections were characterised by "apsolute supremacy of the state media" (''apsolutna prevlast državnih medija'').<ref name=":31" /><ref name=":64">{{Cite book |last=Matić |first=Jovanka |url=https://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/MEDIJI%20I%20IZBORI.pdf |title=Mediji i izbori |date=March 2012 |publisher=[[CeSID]] |isbn=978-86-83491-10-0 |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=Media and Elections |archive-date=21 June 2024 |access-date=15 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240621033051/https://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/MEDIJI%20I%20IZBORI.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Rp|page=19, 23}} "Since the first pluralist parliamentary elections in 1990, the media presentation of the elections was not adapted to the needs of the voters, but to the needs of the parties, and above all to the needs of the ruling party" (''Od prvih pluralističkih parlamentarnih izbora 1990. godine, medijska prezentacija izbora nije bila prilagođena potrebama birača, već potrebama partija, i pre svega potrebama vladajuće partije''), Matić said.<ref name=":64" />{{Rp|page=19}} Opposition parties were also faced with a media blockade; [[RTV Studio B|Studio B]] and [[Radio B92]] were the sole opposition-friendly media during the 1990 elections.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=77}}<ref name=":30" /><ref name=":34" /> Both, however, were limited to the Belgrade region.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=77}}<ref name=":90">{{Cite news |last=Miladinović |first=Aleksandar |date=9 March 2021 |title=Deveti mart u Beogradu: "Dan sa više suza nego suzavca" |trans-title=9 March in Belgrade: "A day with more tears than tear gas" |url=https://www.bbc.com/serbian/lat/srbija-56288602 |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=sr |archive-date=20 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230120220502/https://www.bbc.com/serbian/lat/srbija-56288602 |url-status=live }}</ref> SPS controlled the majority of television and radio stations, as well as newspapers.<ref name=":34" /><ref name=":39" /> During the campaign, SPS used the media to attack their political opponents and discredit presidential candidates, particularly Drašković.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=83|pages=}}<ref name=":50" /><ref name=":52">{{Cite news |last=Silber |first=Laura |date=9 December 1990 |title=Free elections in Yugoslav republic pit nationalists against Communists |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/407096886 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |pages=2A|id={{ProQuest|407096886}} }}</ref> Mićunović, unlike Drašković, was not a presidential candidate and hence faced fewer media attacks.<ref name="Martinov2000" />{{Rp|page=29}}

The state media portrayed SPS as a state-building party, the sole advocate of Serbia's national interests, and the party of peace.<ref name=":64" />{{Rp|page=28}} They often portrayed Milošević and SPS as being identified with the state; this gave Milošević and other government officials a lot of publicity during the campaign.<ref name=":64" />{{Rp|page=28}} On the other hand, the opposition was portrayed as "promoters of war, conflict, bloodshed, and violence" (''zagovornik rata, sukoba, krvoprolića, nasilja'') and offering "chaos and mindlessness, destruction, political dependence on others, regression, and return to the past" (''haos i bezumlje, razaranje, političku zavisnost od drugih i nazadovanje, vraćanje u prošlost'').<ref name=":64" />{{Rp|page=29}} The government called opposition politicians as "enemies of society" (''neprijateljima društva'').<ref name=":33" />

RTB presented candidates for the parliamentary and presidential elections during the election campaign.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=83|pages=}}<ref name=":33" /><ref>{{Cite journal |date=23 October 1990 |title=Odluka o davanju saglasnosti na pravila za predstavljanje kandidata za poslanike u Narodnoj skupštini Republike Srbije, predsednika republike i njihovih izbornih programa |trans-title=Decision on giving consent to the rules for the presentation of candidates for deputies in the National Assembly of the Republic of Serbia, the President of the Republic and their election programmes |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=6697 |journal=Official Gazette of the Republic of Serbia |language=sr |volume=6 |pages=182 |issn=0353-8389}}</ref> Candidates were given 90 minutes of television airtime on RTB, with the first 30 allocated minutes reserved for how candidates wished to present themselves.<ref name=":89" />{{Rp|page=63}}<ref name=":39" /> There was another television show on RTB that focused on campaign activities.<ref name=":89" />{{Rp|page=63}} It was noted that Đurić's presentation in November was cut from 30 minutes to 17 minutes due to a "musical interlude" and "the presenter's lengthy introduction".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Branson |first=Louise |date=8 December 1990 |title=Communists' Grip Besets Critical Serbian Voting |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/278315469 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Newsday]]|id={{ProQuest|278315469}} }}</ref> Mila Štula, a RTB journalist, also characterised Drašković as "a man with a clerical and [[Khomeini]] mentality who is also unsuitable for the head of state because he has no children" (''čoveka pisarskog i homeinijevskog mentaliteta, koji je uz to nepogodan za vođu države jer nema dece'').<ref name=":64" />{{Rp|page=26}} During Drašković's presentation on 2 December, RTB turned his words around; Drašković said, "when we come to power, we will provide minimum conditions for people, primarily pensioners, for a normal life. For example, when those people die, they do not even have money for a coffin; they cannot be buried like people, which is a very sad truth" (''Mi ćemo, kada dođemo na vlast, obezbediti minimalne uslove ljudima, pre svega penzionerima za normalan život. Na primer, ti ljudi kada umru nemaju para ni za kovčeg, ne mogu da se maltene sahrane kao ljudi, što je vrlo tužna istina''), while RTB interpreted his presentation as him wanting to provide all pensioners with coffins. Drašković's comments received backlash from the public.<ref name="Martinov2000" />{{Rp|page=29}} Štula also accused Drašković of owning a villa.<ref name=":75">{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=20 December 2010 |title=Drašković Milošević daje milion glasova "fore" |trans-title=Drašković gives Milošević a million votes time to go |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/draskovic-milosevicu-daje-milion-glasova-fore/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr |archive-date=15 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240715175204/https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/draskovic-milosevicu-daje-milion-glasova-fore/ |url-status=live }}</ref> While Milošević was invited to give a presentation on RTB, he refused to do so because the timeslot for his presentation would replace the play {{Ill|Solunci govore|sr|Solunci govore|italic=yes}} (Thessalonians speak).<ref name=":74" />

A group of 200 journalists from state-controlled media protested on 6 December, demanding press freedom and the resignation of senior officials from RTB and [[Politika a.d.|Politika Publishing House]].<ref name=":89" />{{Rp|page=55}}<ref name=":85">{{Cite news |last=Landay |first=Jonathan S. |date=6 December 1990 |title=About 200 journalists of Serbia's communist-controlled media Thursday protested |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1990/12/06/About-200-journalists-of-Serbias-communist-controlled-media-Thursday-protested/9625660459600/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[United Press International]]}}</ref> Miloš Vasić, a former ''[[Politika]]'' journalist, said that the "news coverage has been especially distorted during campaigning" and that "[Ante] Marković's party was completely ignored".<ref name=":85" />

=== Issues ===
The campaign focused on issues such as Kosovo, nationalism, the transformation of the Yugoslav federation,<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=78}} social and economic prosperity, and democracy.<ref name=":30" /><ref name=":51" /><ref name=":52" /> Throughout the campaign, opposition parties focused on ideological themes.<ref name=":30" /> Milošević and Drašković pledged to reform the economy in line with Western standards.<ref name=":48" /> However, economic issues were not central to the campaign; rather, nationalism was the primary issue.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=83|pages=}}<ref name=":51" /><ref name=":60">{{Cite book |last=Mihailović |first=Srećko |url=https://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/OKO%20IZBORA%2015.pdf |title=Oko izbora 15: Parlamentarni izbori u Republici Srbiji 21. januara 2007. |date=July 2007 |publisher=[[CeSID]] |isbn=978-86-83491-43-8 |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=Oko izbora 15: Parliamentary elections in the Republic of Serbia on 21 January 2007 |archive-date=21 June 2024 |access-date=14 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240621024924/https://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/OKO%20IZBORA%2015.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Rp|page=11}} The campaign was met with high social polarisation and fear, as well as politically motivated incidents.<ref name=":19" /><ref name=":39" /><ref name=":40">{{Cite news |last=Harden |first=Blaine |date=11 December 1990 |title=Ex-Communists Retain Power in Serbian Vote; Outcome Fuels Fears of Yugoslav Breakup |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/307313472 |access-date=11 July 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |pages=A8|id={{ProQuest|307313472}} }}</ref> Slobodan Ivanović, an SPO activist, was killed in [[Dubnica (Vranje)|Dubnica]], near [[Vranje]], by a SPS supporter while campaigning just before the campaign ended on 5 December.<ref name=":19" /><ref name=":30" /><ref name=":74" /> Drašković accused SPS of attempting to instigate a civil war following the murder.<ref name=":74" />

In the October 1990 opinion poll done  by the [[Institute for Political Studies in Belgrade]], 33% of the respondents ranked living standards and economic development as their important issues, with preservation of Yugoslavia coming in second.<ref name="IDN_October1990">{{cite web |last1=Klingemann |first1=Hans-Dieter |last2=Ljiljana |first2=Baćević |date=1997 |title=Izborna Studija, Srbija 1990 (1. talas) |trans-title=Election Study Serbia 1990 (1st Wave) |url=https://search.gesis.org/research_data/ZA2901 |access-date=20 July 2024 |website=[[GESIS – Leibniz Institute for the Social Sciences]] |doi=10.4232/1.2901}}</ref> The majority of respondents wanted to remain in a federal Yugoslavia.<ref name="IDN_October1990" /> In November, 35% of the respondents said that living standards and economic development were their most important issues.<ref name="IDN_November1990">{{cite web |last1=Klingemann |first1=Hans-Dieter |last2=Ljiljana |first2=Baćević |date=1997 |title=Izborna Studija, Srbija 1990 (2. talas) |trans-title=Election Study Serbia 1990 (2nd Wave) |url=https://search.gesis.org/research_data/ZA2902 |access-date=20 July 2024 |website=[[GESIS – Leibniz Institute for the Social Sciences]] |doi=10.4232/1.2902 |archive-date=20 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240720150952/https://search.gesis.org/research_data/ZA2902 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the December poll, this issue was agreed upon by 39% of the respondents, with environmental issues ranking second.<ref name="IDN_December1990">{{cite web |last1=Klingemann |first1=Hans-Dieter |last2=Ljiljana |first2=Baćević |date=1997 |title=Izborna Studija, Srbija 1990 (3. talas) |trans-title=Election Study Serbia 1990 (3rd Wave) |url=https://search.gesis.org/research_data/ZA2903 |access-date=20 July 2024 |website=[[GESIS – Leibniz Institute for the Social Sciences]] |doi=10.4232/1.2903 |archive-date=20 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240720155301/https://search.gesis.org/research_data/ZA2903 |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Timeline ===
On 12 September, UOS held a protest in Belgrade, which was attended by about 50,000 demonstrators.<ref name=":88" />{{Rp|page=55}} They demanded a ninety-day election campaign, more television airtime for opposition parties, and representation of the opposition in bodies that oversee the elections.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=71}} At the end of the protest, Šešelj and his supporters tried to seize the protest.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=72}} Once the election was called, SPO organised a protest at [[Kosovo Polje]].<ref name=":30" /> Due to SPS supporters organising a counter-protest, violence was on the verge of happening, though the police intervened and the protest remained peaceful.<ref name=":30" />

Throughout the campaign period, there were discussions about orchestrating an election boycott.<ref name=":26" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=29 September 1990 |title=Communist Grip on Serbia Tightened |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/281256028 |access-date=13 July 2024 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |pages=15|id={{ProQuest|281256028}} }}</ref> Drašković and his SPO were the first to initiate a boycott on 2 October.<ref name=":26" /><ref name=":76">{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=13 December 2010 |title=Nesložna opozicija i bahati režim – 1990. godine |trans-title=Discordant opposition and arrogant regime – 1990 |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/neslozna-opozicija-i-bahati-rezim-1990-godine/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr}}</ref> SPO proclaimed a boycott on the grounds that the election would be unfair, and demanded that it be rescheduled for 27 December.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=72}}<ref>{{Cite news |last=Đurić |first=Nešo |date=3 October 1990 |title=Serbian opposition threatens election boycott |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1990/10/03/Serbian-opposition-threatens-election-boycott/1805654926400/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[United Press International]]}}</ref> Mićunović and [[Zoran Đinđić]] were opposed to a boycott; Mićunović said that, in case of a boycott, opposition parties would "leave a clear space for the existing government, and we would deprive our own sympathisers of the opportunity to choose" (''ostavljamo čist prostor postojećoj vlasti, a sopstvene simpatizere lišavamo mogućnosti da biraju'').<ref name=":26" /> After DS decided to take part in the elections, Čavoški left the party; he formed the [[Serbian Liberal Party]] in 1991.<ref name=":26" /> Đinđić succeeded Čavoški as the president of the executive board of DS.<ref name="Tomic-2010">{{Cite web |last=Tomić |first=N. |date=17 December 2010 |title=Sve skupštine demokrata |trans-title=All assemblies of the Democrats |url=https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/sve-skupstine-demokrata/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230120212100/https://www.danas.rs/vesti/politika/sve-skupstine-demokrata/ |archive-date=20 January 2023 |access-date=20 January 2023 |website=[[Danas (newspaper)|Danas]] |language=sr }}</ref> [[Dragan Veselinov]], the president of NSS, said that his party would take part in the elections, while the NRS was also opposed to a boycott.<ref name=":88" />{{Rp|page=56}}<ref name=":77">{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=16 December 2010 |title=Izbori kao "lutkarsko pozorište" |trans-title=Elections as a "puppet theater" |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/izbori-kao-lutkarsko-pozoriste/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr |archive-date=18 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240718201155/https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/izbori-kao-lutkarsko-pozoriste/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In-mid November, discussions about an election boycott intensified.<ref name=":77" /> The government refused to implement the opposition's revisions to the election law, which included demands such as representation of the opposition in RIK and local commissions.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=81|pages=}} Initially, 11 parties announced in mid-November that they would boycott the elections; the number soon increased to 19.<ref name=":77" /> Most opposition parties, including SPO, DS, SRSJ, SNO, NRS, ND, and LS, together with presidential candidates Drašković and Đurić, proclaimed an election boycott and withdrew their candidatures.<ref name=":33" /><ref name=":26" /><ref name=":58">{{Cite news |last=Fish |first=Jim |date=24 November 1990 |title=Serbian Opposition to Boycott Election |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/307374191 |access-date=14 July 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |pages=A18 |id={{ProQuest|307374191}}}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Radovanović |first1=I. |last2=Katić |first2=G. |last3=Šutalo |first3=M. |date=24–25 November 1990 |title=Opozicija bojkotuje izbore! |trans-title=The opposition is boycotting the elections! |url=https://istorijskenovine.unilib.rs/view/index.html#issue:UB_00064_19901124 |access-date=3 August 2024 |work=[[Borba (newspaper)|Borba]] |pages=13 |language=sr |archive-date=24 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240324152716/https://istorijskenovine.unilib.rs/view/index.html#issue:UB_00064_19901124 |url-status=live }}</ref> They were also concerned about potential vote fraud if they chose to participate.<ref>{{Cite news |date=3 December 1990 |title=How Yugoslavia Can Hang Together |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/108436626 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[New York Times]] |pages=A18 |id={{ProQuest|108436626}}}}</ref> By 25 November, around 40 parties had announced their decision to boycott the election.<ref>{{Cite news |date=27 November 1990 |title=Serbian Communists panic at mass boycott of election |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/436291369 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Toronto Star]] |pages=A15 |id={{ProQuest|436291369}} |archive-date=6 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240806214934/https://www.proquest.com/docview/436291369 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=26 November 1990 |title=Može li Skupština promeniti odluku |trans-title=Can the Assembly change the decision? |url=https://istorijskenovine.unilib.rs/view/index.html#issue:UB_00064_19901126 |access-date=3 August 2024 |work=[[Borba (newspaper)|Borba]] |pages=1 |language=sr |archive-date=24 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240324152716/https://istorijskenovine.unilib.rs/view/index.html#issue:UB_00064_19901126 |url-status=live }}</ref> The government of Serbia believed that a boycott would undermine international acceptance of the election results; Milošević also criticised parties that proclaimed the boycott. He accused the "rightist conservatives and often dark forces" (''desnih konzervativnih i često sasvim mračnih snaga'') of trying to bring Serbia "to the past, to bring it into chaos" (''u prošlost, da je uvuku u haos'').<ref name=":79">{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=17 December 2020 |title=U redu – evo vam amandmani! |trans-title=OK – here are the amendments! |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/u-redu-evo-vam-amandmani/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr |archive-date=15 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240715173656/https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/u-redu-evo-vam-amandmani/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The government eventually accepted the recommendations on 26 November, and the boycott was cancelled.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=81|pages=}}<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Milošević |first1=Milan |last2=Ninčić |first2=Roksanda |last3=Mladenović |first3=Filip |last4=Cerović |first4=Stojan |date=8 October 2019 |title=Srbija u poslednjoj jednopartijskoj nedelji |trans-title=Serbia in the last one-party week |url=https://vreme.com/vreme/srbija-u-poslednjoj-jednopartijskoj-nedelji/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[Vreme]] |language=sr |archive-date=20 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240720132612/https://vreme.com/vreme/srbija-u-poslednjoj-jednopartijskoj-nedelji/ |url-status=live }}</ref> However, the government refused to postpone the elections to 23 December or permit overseas Serbians to vote.<ref name=":88" />{{Rp|page=59}}<ref>{{Cite news |last=Đurić |first=Nešo |date=26 November 1990 |title=Communist government succumbs to opposition pressure |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1990/11/26/Communist-government-succumbs-to-opposition-pressure/4121659595600/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[United Press International]]}}</ref> Drašković said that "some effort was made towards a reasonable solution" (''učinjen je izvestan napor ka razumnom rešenju'') and that SPO would not boycott the elections.<ref name=":79" />

==== Serbian Renewal Movement ====
Drašković, a right-wing nationalist,<ref name=":33" /><ref name=":34" /> portrayed the election as a struggle "between good and evil".<ref name=":27">{{Cite news |last=Bohlen |first=Celestine |date=11 December 1990 |title=Ruling party wins Serbian elections |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/12/11/world/ruling-party-wins-serbian-elections.html |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]] |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=20 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231120003748/https://www.nytimes.com/1990/12/11/world/ruling-party-wins-serbian-elections.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He earned the moniker "King of the Squares" (''kralj trgova'') for his inflammatory and provocative rhetoric during the election campaign;<ref name=":30" /><ref name=":49" /> his public image was also compared to [[Rasputin]] because of his beard and hair.<ref name=":47" /> His campaign events were described as a "mixture of Rasputin and [[rock concerts]]".<ref name=":50" /> During the campaign, Drašković took a tough approach towards Kosovo and Serbs in other Yugoslav constituent republics.<ref name=":48" /><ref name=":53">{{Cite news |last=Smith |first=Tony |date=10 December 1990 |title=Fraud Charged in Yugoslav Vote |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/294564049 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[The Boston Globe]] |pages=2 |id={{ProQuest|294564049}}}}</ref> He also backed free-market capitalism and the restoration of the monarchy.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=83|pages=}} He proposed a "seven-day solution" for Kosovo, requiring Kosovo Albanians to sign a "Serbian loyalty oath or get out [of Kosovo]" or face military intervention.<ref name=":51" /> During his campaign rally in early October in [[Novi Pazar]], he criticised Muslims and Croats.<ref name=":78">{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=14 December 2010 |title=Ruke, barjaci i prvi raskoli |trans-title=Arms, flags and the first schisms |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/ruke-barjaci-i-prvi-raskoli/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr}}</ref> "Anyone who, in this land of [[Raška (region)|Raška]], holds a Turkish flag, an Ustaša flag, an Albanian flag, or anyone else's flag except the Serbian flag, will be left without a hand and without a flag" (''svaki onaj ko u ruku, ovom Raškom zemljom, stegne turski barjak, ustaški barjak, albanski barjak ili bilo čiji drugi barjak sem srpskog, ostaće i bez ruke i bez barjaka''), Drašković said.<ref name=":30" /> During the same period, he urged sending paramilitary units during the [[Log Revolution]] in Croatia.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Fish |first=Jim |date=2 October 1990 |title=Civil War Threatens in Yugoslavia; Serb Minority Claims Oppression in Croatia |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/307356039 |access-date=13 July 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |pages=A12|id={{ProQuest|307356039}} }}</ref>

In early November, Yugoslav Prime Minister Ante Marković and his SRSJ criticized the SPO, labeling it a greater threat than Milošević.<ref name=":77" /> In late November, Drašković accused Milošević of destabilising Yugoslavia, but dismissed allegations that SPO would overthrow the government.<ref name=":75" /><ref name=":80">{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=22 December 2010 |title=Šećeroski brži od predizborne tišine |trans-title=Šećeroski is faster than pre-election silence |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/seceroski-brzi-od-predizborne-tisine/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr |archive-date=15 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240715173656/https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/seceroski-brzi-od-predizborne-tisine/ |url-status=live }}</ref> By the end of the campaign, Drašković moderated his rhetoric,<ref name=":51" /><ref name=":41">{{Cite news |date=10 December 1990 |title=Fraud alleged as Serbs hold first free vote in 50 years |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/436315015 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[Toronto Star]] |pages=A12 |id={{ProQuest|436315015}} |archive-date=11 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240711175053/https://www.proquest.com/docview/436315015 |url-status=live }}</ref> and said that he would negotiate with Croatia and Slovenia to avoid Yugoslavia's disintegration.<ref name=":39" /><ref name=":27" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=9 December 1990 |title=Yugoslavia's vote could lead to secession move |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/418258333 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Star Tribune]] |pages=10A |id={{ProQuest|418258333}}}}</ref> While he was opposed to the breakup of Yugoslavia,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ferguson |first=Alan |date=20 October 1990 |title=Yugoslavia risks religious war, author says |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/436292329 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Toronto Star]], [[Reuters]] |pages=D5 |id={{ProQuest|436292329}}}}</ref><ref name=":46">{{Cite news |last=Reaves |first=Joseph A. |date=7 November 1990 |title=Yugoslav elections may be last hope of averting a civil war |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/283098590 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |pages=15 |id={{ProQuest|283098590}}}}</ref> he favoured the concept of [[Greater Serbia]].<ref name=":67" />{{Rp|pages=|page=12}} Drašković's last campaign event, attended by 15,000 people, was a commemorative rally for the murdered SPO activist.<ref name=":30" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=7 December 1990 |title=Huge rally sets stage for vote in Serbia |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/239551134 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Ottawa Citizen]] |pages=A9 |id={{ProQuest|239551134}}}}</ref>

==== Democratic Party ====
[[File:Predstavljanje kandidata Demokratske stranke izbori 1990.jpg|thumb|alt=Election posters of candidates of the Democratic Party in the 1990 parliamentary election, featuring Slobodan Vučković, Vojislav Koštunica, Marko Janković, and Miodrag Perišić|DS (four candidates pictured) campaigned on an economically liberal and pro-Western programme during the campaign]]Mićunović stated that DS campaigned with limited infrastructure and was forced to use only direct methods of campaigning.<ref name=":31" /> DS campaigned on a technocratic, economically liberal-oriented, and pro-Western programme.<ref name=":34" /><ref name=":39" /><ref name=":51" /> In addition, they favoured a representative parliamentary democracy, as well as human and political freedoms and civic rights.<ref name="Vukomanović">{{cite book |author=Vukomanović |first=Dijana |author-link=Dijana Vukomanović |url=http://izbornareforma.rs/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/resurscentar/Ideologija%20i%20politicke%20stranke%20u%20Srbiji.pdf |title=Ideologija i političke stranke u Srbiji |date=2007 |publisher=[[Friedrich Ebert Stiftung]]; [[Faculty of Political Science in Sarajevo|Fakultet političkih nauka u Sarajevu]]; Institut Društvenih Nauka |isbn=978-86-83767-23-6 |editor-last=Lutovac |editor-first=Zoran |editor-link=Zoran Lutovac |location=Belgrade |page=92 |language=sr |trans-title=Ideology and political parties in Serbia |chapter=Ideološke matrice političkih partija u Srbiji (1990{{endash}}2007) |trans-chapter=Ideological matrices of political parties in Serbia (1990{{endash}}2007) |oclc=298827370 |access-date=2 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131127041235/http://izbornareforma.rs/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/resurscentar/Ideologija%20i%20politicke%20stranke%20u%20Srbiji.pdf |archive-date=27 November 2013 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Furthermore, they took a moderate approach towards Kosovo, opting to negotiate with Kosovo Albanians and wishing to preserve the Yugoslav federation.<ref name=":48" /><ref name=":67" />{{Rp|pages=|page=12}} DS was a [[catch-all party]] and comprised ideologically diverse groupings, and unlike SPO, it did not use populist rhetoric.<ref name=":67" />{{Rp|pages=|page=12}}<ref name="Vukadinovic-20022" /> Political scientist Vukašin Pavlović and [[Metropolitan University Prague]] lecturer Marko Stojić positioned DS on the centre-right during the 1990 elections.<ref name=":110" />{{Rp|page=143}}<ref name="Stojic-2018">{{Cite book |last=Stojić |first=Marko |url=|title=Party Responses to the EU in the Western Balkans: Transformation, Opposition or Defiance? |date=2018 |publisher=[[Palgrave Macmillan]] |isbn=978-3-319-59563-4 |location=Cham, Switzerland |page=162 |lccn=2017948171 |oclc=1003200383}}</ref> DS received support from intellectuals during the election.<ref name=":48" /><ref name=":89" />{{Rp|page=44}}

Đinđić said in early November that DS would concentrate on issues such as reprivatisation of the economy, personal freedoms and rights of citizens, and the issue of Serbia within Yugoslavia.<ref name=":77" /> DS did not campaign on nationalist themes.<ref name=":67" />{{Rp|pages=|page=11}} [[Vojislav Koštunica]], the vice-president of DS, described the elections as "vital" and said that "without free elections, nothing can change in Yugoslavia".<ref name=":46" /> At the end of the campaign, [[Vladeta Janković]] said that DS would enter the government led by SPO in the event of a SPO victory.<ref name=":51" />

==== Socialist Party of Serbia ====
SPS campaigned on a constructive agenda and positive themes, portraying itself as a [[democratic socialist]] party.<ref name=":30" /><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Hoare |first=Marko Attila |date=2016 |title=Slobodan Milošević's Place in Serbian History |url=https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/0265691406065446?etoc= |url-status=live |journal=European History Quarterly |volume=36 |issue=3 |pages=454 |doi=10.1177/0265691406065446 |issn=0265-6914 |s2cid=144194276 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230108182008/https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/0265691406065446?etoc= |archive-date=8 January 2023 |access-date=8 January 2023|url-access=subscription }}</ref> In its first programme, issued in October, SPS stated that "Serbia as a [[Socialist state|socialist republic]], founded on law and social justice" was its goal.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=64}}<ref name=":210">{{cite book |author=Branković |first=Srbobran |url=https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003063629 |title=The Communist Successor Parties of Central and Eastern Europe |publisher=Routledge |year=2002 |isbn=978-1-003-06362-9 |editor-last1=Bozóki |editor-first1=András |location=New York |pages= |chapter=The Yugoslav "Left" Parties |doi=10.4324/9781003063629 |access-date=2 January 2023 |editor-last2=Ishiyama |editor-first2=John T |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230111141822/https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/edit/10.4324/9781003063629/communist-successor-parties-central-eastern-europe-john-ishiyama-andras-bozoki |archive-date=11 January 2023 |url-status=live |s2cid=154321284 }}</ref>{{Rp|page=206}} Nevertheless, SPS relied heavily on a combination of nationalism and social demagogy in its rhetoric.<ref name=":33" /> Unlike other former communist parties, SPS did not abandon its [[authoritarian]] traits.<ref name=":33" /> SPS also promoted [[conspiracy theories]] during the campaign, such as the claim of a United States plot to destroy Serbia.<ref name=":52" /> Their president, Milošević, campaigned on presenting himself as a moderate, promising stability, defending Serbia's national interests, and preventing unemployment among industrial workers.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=82|pages=}}<ref name=":39" /><ref name=":27" /> Opposition parties criticised Milošević due to his former affiliation with SKS.<ref name=":27" /> Milošević advocated for maintaining the [[status quo]] and said that a [[civil war]] could break out if other constituent republics declared independence.<ref name=":27" /> Most of his supporters were frightened of a change to a [[market economy]], political change, and Yugoslavia's breakup, while he opposed [[Free-market|free-market policies]] and promoted [[protectionist]] measures instead.<ref name=":30" /><ref name=":27" /> During the campaign period in October, the government of Serbia raised tariffs and introduced trade barriers to reduce imports from Croatia and Slovenia.<ref name=":27" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=24 October 1990 |title=Serbians kill internal market |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/251788378 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Edmonton Journal]], [[Associated Press]] |pages=B16|id={{ProQuest|251788378}} }}</ref>

While campaigning in early November, Milošević criticised the opposition: "That path to a modern society and a developed society is incompatible with any mystical messages, which like ghosts of the past circulate in some parts of Serbia and which various false prophets and madmen offer to the Serbian people as a pledge of the future" (''Taj put u moderno društvo i razvijeno društvo nespojiv je sa bilo kakvim mističkim porukama, koje kao aveti prošlosti kruže u nekim delovima Srbije i koje razni lažni proroci i ludaci nude srpskom narodu kao zalog budućnosti'').<ref>{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=15 December 2010 |title=Ko je "lažni prorok", a ko "ludak"? |trans-title=Who is a "false prophet" and who is a "madman"? |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/ko-je-lazni-prorok-a-ko-ludak/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr}}</ref> Throughout the rest of the month, he intensified his presidential campaign.<ref name=":79" /> He later said that "a Socialist defeat could unleash dark forces from the country's past".<ref name=":39" /> In the final campaign week, Milošević gave a choreographed speech in Novi Sad; workers from state-owned factories were given the afternoon off and sent to Milošević's rally.<ref name=":54">{{Cite news |last=Harden |first=Blaine |date=27 December 1990 |title=Serbia's State Media Help to Inflame Yugoslavia's Rising Ethnic Passions |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/307325444 |access-date=14 July 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |pages=A24 |id={{ProQuest|307325444}}}}</ref> After the rally, the supporters were bussed back home.<ref name=":54" /> Despite only 15,000 people attending the rally, the state-controlled media reported the number to be between 75,000 and 150,000.<ref name=":54" /> His last campaign event was held on 5 December.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Petrovic |first=Barney |date=6 December 1990 |title=Serbian Leader Sees Enemies All Around |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/244598213 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[The Guardian]] |pages=8 |id={{ProQuest|244598213}}}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Landay |first=Jonathan S. |date=5 December 1990 |title=Serbian president wraps up re-election campaign |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1990/12/05/Serbian-president-wraps-up-re-election-campaign/8231660373200/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[United Press International]]}}</ref>

==== Others ====
Among the numerous opposition groups and politicians who rose to prominence during the campaign, the [[Association for the Yugoslav Democratic Initiative]] (UJDI) expressed its opposition to confederalism, instead advocating an accord on the formation of a multi-party Assembly of Yugoslavia.<ref name=":76" /> The nominee of UJDI and SRSJ was [[Ivan Đurić]]; he campaigned on a moderate, liberal, and pro-European programme and received support from the West.<ref name=":33" /><ref name=":48" /><ref name=":67" />{{Rp|pages=|page=18}} Đurić strongly rejected nationalism and favoured a dialogue between Serbs and Albanians.<ref name=":67" />{{Rp|pages=|page=18}}<ref name=":78" /> SRSJ got support exclusively in Vojvodina, but not in the rest of Serbia.<ref name=":39" /> During the campaign period, Šešelj was initially sentenced to prison on 2 October due to offences regarding public order and peace.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=75}}<ref name=":78" /><ref>{{Cite book |last=Sell |first=Louis |title=Slobodan Milosevic and the Destruction of Yugoslavia |date=2002 |publisher=[[Duke University Press]] |isbn=978-0-8223-8525-7 |pages=327}}</ref> He was later released but soon re-imprisoned on October 23 for 45 days for allegedly attempting to recruit volunteers for Croatia's Log Revolution.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=75}} His SČP was not permitted to take part in the elections.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=75}} DS condemned Šešelj's imprisonment.<ref name=":78" /> In late October, [[Nenad Čanak]], the president of [[League of Social Democrats of Vojvodina|League of Social Democrats of Vojvodina/Yugoslavia]], condemned the 1988 anti-bureaucratic revolution in Vojvodina in a campaign interview.<ref name=":78" />

Đurić criticised both the SPS and nationalist anti-communists during the campaign in November, arguing that "if we want to join Europe, [...] we need a moderate, authentic, democratic position" (''ukoliko želimo u Evropu, [...] potrebna nam je umerena, autentična, demokratska pozicija'').<ref name=":79" /> After Šešelj left prison in November, he launched his presidential campaign; he strongly criticised other opposition parties such as SPO.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=75}} However, he thanked DS for supporting his release and said that SČP would consider endorsing the candidates of DS in the parliamentary election.<ref name=":77" /> Shortly before the election, Šešelj emphasised his support for monarchism and named the SRSJ and the [[League of Communists – Movement for Yugoslavia]] as the "two new evils" (''dva nova zla'').<ref name=":75" /> Out of the minor candidates, [[Nikola Šećeroski]], a [[Plastic arts|plastic artist]], received the most attention.<ref name=":80" /> During an interview for RTB, for which he is remembered, Šećeroski said that the municipality of Čukarica accused him of making "brushes and brooms" (''četke i metle''), despite being a plastic artist.<ref name=":80" /> He has been described as a symbol of "ridiculing and making the democratic process meaningless" (''ismevanje i obesmišljavanja demokratskog procesa'').<ref name=":33" /> Ćuić, a driving instructor and poet, was not politically active prior to the election.<ref name=":22" /><ref name=":28">{{Cite news |last=Stevanović |first=Katarina |date=30 March 2022 |title=Izbori u Srbiji i žene: Sve predsedničke kandidatkinje u Srbiji – tradicija koju je počela Ljiljana Ćuić |trans-title=Elections in Serbia and women: All presidential candidates in Serbia – a tradition started by Ljiljana Ćuić |url=https://www.bbc.com/serbian/lat/srbija-60864437 |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=sr}}</ref> In the election, she was nominated on behalf of a citizens group.<ref name=":28" /> Ćuić did not run an active campaign but participated in television programmes.<ref name=":28" /> "Whoever throws a cigarette butt on the street has done evil to all mankind" (''Ko baci opušak na ulicu, učinio je zlo celom čovečanstvu''), Ćuić once said during the campaign.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Čekerevac |first=Mirjana |date=19 March 2017 |title=Svi naši predsednički kandidati |trans-title=All our presidential candidates |url=https://www.politika.rs/sr/clanak/376466/Pogledi/Svi-nasi-predsednicki-kandidati |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=[[Politika]] |language=sr}}</ref> Out of all independent candidates for the parliamentary election, Milićević campaigned on improving [[children's rights]] and the rights of the elderly, as well as expanding the number of kindergartens, schools, and parks.<ref name=":74" /> Subota criticised the poor conditions that independent candidates had to endure during the election campaign.<ref name=":80" />

Veselinov said that opposition forces would unite should a second round of the presidential election take place.<ref name=":24" /> For the second round of the parliamentary elections, opposition parties united under the UOS banner.<ref name=":68" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=26 December 1990 |title=Former Communists in Serbia Win Majority in Parliamentary Vote |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/108416582 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]], [[Associated Press]] |pages=A3|id={{ProQuest|108416582}} }}</ref><ref name=":69">{{Cite news |last=Kojić |first=Nikola |date=17 April 2023 |title=Kad se opozicija ujedini: Sve opozicione koalicije u Srbiji od 1990. godine |trans-title=When the opposition unites: All opposition coalitions in Serbia since 1990 |url=https://n1info.rs/vesti/kad-se-opozicija-ujedini-sve-opozicione-koalicije-u-srbiji-od-1990-godine/ |access-date=15 July 2024 |work=[[N1 (TV channel)|N1]] |language=sr}}</ref> UOS was officially formalised on 11 December.<ref name=":88" />{{Rp|page=60}} Its members included SPO, DS, SRSJS, NSS, NS, ND, NRS, and UJDI.<ref name=":69" /> UOS called for its voters to vote for opposition candidates in the second round "regardless of their political affiliation" (''nezavisno od njegove stranačke pripadnosti'').<ref name=":69" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Đurić |first=Nešo |date=12 December 1990 |title=Opposition mounts united effort to block communist majority |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1990/12/12/Opposition-mounts-united-effort-to-block-communist-majority/4345660978000/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[United Press International]]}}</ref> Due to its heterogeneous nature, UOS only advocated for the establishment of parliamentarism and the introduction of a proportional representation system.<ref name=":88" />{{Rp|page=60}} Šešelj declined to join the coalition, though he personally endorsed DS candidates.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=24 December 2010 |title=Srećna nova 1991! |trans-title=Happy New Year 1991! |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/srecna-nova-1991/ |access-date=15 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr |archive-date=15 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240715173655/https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/srecna-nova-1991/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Opinion polls ==
{{Main|Opinion polling for the 1990 Serbian general election}}
[[Opinion polling]] for the 1990 general election in Serbia was conducted by the [[Institute for Political Studies in Belgrade]], Institute of Social Sciences and Centre for Political Research, and one unknown independent polling firm.<ref name=":44">{{Cite journal |last=Mihailović |first=Srećko |date=1990 |title=Sondaža javnog mnjenja uoči prvih višestranačkih izbora u Srbiji |trans-title=Public opinion survey before the first multi-party elections in Serbia |journal=[[Sociološki pregled]] |language=sr |issue=1 |issn=0085-6320}}</ref>{{rp|pages=|page=19}}{{efn|Institute of Social Sciences and Centre for Political Research polls:
*December 1990<ref name="IDN_December1990" />
*November 1990<ref name="IDN_November1990" />
*October 1990<ref name="IDN_October1990" />
Institute for Political Studies in Belgrade poll:
*December 1990<ref>{{cite news |date=5 December 1990 |title=Istraživanja: SPS i Milošević |trans-title=Research: SPS and Milošević |work=[[Politika ekspres]] |page=1 |language=sr |issue=9606 |issn=0032-3381}}</ref>
Unknown independent polling firm poll:
*September 1990<ref name=":67" />{{Rp|pages=|page=13}}}} According to ''Washington Post'', opinion polls were not very reliable during the 1990 elections.<ref name=":51" />

