== Conduct ==
The election observers included 170 international journalists and 65 members from monitoring organisations.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Sullivan |first=Betsy |date=9 December 1990 |title=Byzantine political mix comes to boil in Serbia |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/287741551 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[The Plain Dealer]] |id={{ProQuest|287741551}}}}</ref> Despite the opposition's claims of [[electoral fraud]], election observers gave the elections a transitional rating and declared them free.<ref name=":30" /><ref name=":33" /><ref name=":41" /> RIK dismissed the allegations of voter fraud.<ref>{{Cite news |date=11 December 1990 |title=Walesa Wins, Pledges to Rebuild Poland |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/291195854 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[The Christian Science Monitor]] |pages=6|id={{ProQuest|291195854}} }}</ref> The opposition later conceded, expressing surprise and disappointment with the outcome.<ref name=":40" /><ref name=":43">{{Cite news |last=Williams |first=Carol J. |date=10 December 1990 |title=Serbian Incumbents Headed for an Election Victory Yugoslavia: Former Communists also appear to have done well in Montenegro |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/281250588 |access-date=11 July 2024 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |pages=8|id={{ProQuest|281250588}} }}</ref>

According to the [[National Republican Institute for International Affairs]], the election was fair, but observers complained about "bias and favouritism in media coverage".<ref name=":40" /> Zoran Hodzera, who monitored on behalf of the Americans and Canadians of Serbia delegation, stated that the "elections would not be considered legal in the West" due to irregularities that occurred.<ref name=":55">{{Cite news |date=27 December 1990 |title=Serbia's first free election marred by accusations |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/275117303 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Waterloo Region Record]], [[Associated Press]] |pages=B5|id={{ProQuest|275117303}} }}</ref> Matić said that the election was held under unequal conditions.<ref name=":89" />{{Rp|page=47}} Political scientist Nebojša Vladisaljević argued that the election was competitive but neither free nor fair.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Vučićević |first1=Dušan |title=Kako, koga i zašto smo birali: izbori u Srbiji 1990–2020. godine |last2=Jovanović |first2=Milan |last3=Vladisaljević |first3=Nebojša |date=2020 |publisher=[[Official Gazette (Serbia)|Official Gazette]] |isbn=978-86-7419-332-7 |location=Belgrade |pages=8 |language=sr |trans-title=How, whom and why we elected: elections in Serbia 1990-2020 |chapter=Izbori, demokratija i takmičarski autoritarizam u Srbiji 1990-2020. |trans-chapter=Elections, democracy and competitive authoritarianism in Serbia 1990–2020 |chapter-url=https://d1wqtxts1xzle7.cloudfront.net/64695895/Vladisavljevic_Izbori_demokratija_i_takmicarski_autoritarizam_u_Srbiji_1990_2020_-libre.pdf?1602857508=&response-content-disposition=inline%3B+filename%3DIzbori_demokratija_i_takmicarski_autorit.pdf&Expires=1721078740&Signature=fUC3F~Ubmmjey2lewfN0AZmx0-WcrFBAnM9itzvLVmq86OAK4xSpAxTQ-4PKUxgxVIB2uf7vA~nwQ8UqDp41ip2dFRZE6auj0Q6yT3lsAokKrfiZeCb2M65NM-rbJ~q4hfq3JdUBAHOl6vq9jY9J80S4a2Kk9NRLIaNATUaw0XfFgwfmqGtLkGH6JMFctoKnpAZ2tEvaFiZhMbeak9qLph0nKX5khsqcAcjcrykxWH9N2zZk7DzvMeNg96kZxYaFb7xQ03fWgFU0KFYNzDhDrCituIdPaMlVQAb8kS8zKZyW-Elpt9m3uWhGNRodz6BO2gzY~x1rD6YZkB~OHDY2Dg__&Key-Pair-Id=APKAJLOHF5GGSLRBV4ZA}}</ref> 

Claims of electoral fraud included ballot stuffing, abuse of postal votes, manipulation in vote counting, and attempts to undermine the credibility of the elections.<ref name=":30" /><ref name=":33" /> Hodzera said that the number of voters increased immediately before the elections, some voters voted without their ID cards, the government intimidated voters, and state-run media was biased against the opposition during the campaign.<ref name=":55" /> The newspaper ''[[Vreme]]'' also made similar observations, in addition to ballot stuffing and the inclusion of deceased voters on the electoral roll.<ref>{{Cite news |date=24 December 1990 |title=Država pod sumnjom |trans-title=State under suspicion |url=https://old.vreme.com/cms/view.php?id=1391540 |access-date=19 July 2024 |work=[[Vreme]] |pages=42 |language=sr}}</ref> Political scientist Vladimir Goati reported that while the irregularities occurred – which included unauthorised changes to the electoral roll which favoured SPS candidates – only a few were recorded.<ref name=":73">{{Cite book |last1=Orlović |first1=Slaviša |title=Trideset godina obnovljenog višepartizma u Srbiji – (ne)naučene lekcije |last2=Kovačević |first2=Despot |date=2020 |publisher=[[University of Belgrade]], [[Centre for Democracy (Serbia)|Centre for Democracy]], [[Hanns Seidel Foundation]] |isbn=978-86-6425-069-6 |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=Thirty years of renewed multipartyism in Serbia - (un)learned lessons}}</ref>{{Rp|page=12}} According to political scientist [[Dušan Pavlović (economist)|Dušan Pavlović]], vote buying was the most widespread during the 1990 elections.<ref name=":73" />{{Rp|page=216|pages=}}

== Results ==
Unlike in most post-communist countries after the Revolutions of 1989 and constituent republics of Yugoslavia, the elections in Serbia were won by the incumbent.<ref name=":59" />{{Rp|page=11}}<ref>{{Cite news |date=13 December 1990 |title=Serbia re-elects Communist leader |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/239548444 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Ottawa Citizen]], [[Reuters]] |pages=A11|id={{ProQuest|239548444}} }}</ref> In comparison, the [[1990 Slovenian parliamentary election|elections in Slovenia]], [[1990 Croatian parliamentary election|Croatia]], [[1990 Bosnian general election|Bosnia and Herzegovina]], and [[1990 Macedonian parliamentary election|Macedonia]] were won by anti-communists.<ref name=":58" /><ref name=":32">{{Cite magazine |last=Borrell |first=John |date=6 August 1990 |title=Yugoslavia: The Old Demons Arise |url=https://content.time.com/time/subscriber/article/0,33009,970851,00.html |access-date=10 July 2024 |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |issn=0040-781X |archive-date=2 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230102002522/https://content.time.com/time/subscriber/article/0,33009,970851,00.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=29 November 1990 |title=Yugoslav civil war called likely |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/251701586 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Edmonton Journal]] |pages=B13|id={{ProQuest|251701586}} }}</ref> RIK published preliminary results of the elections on 10 December.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Landay |first=Jonathan S. |date=10 December 1990 |title=First results put communists ahead in Serbia, Montenegro |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1990/12/10/First-results-putcommunists-ahead-in-Serbia-Montenegro/2799660805200/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[United Press International]]}}</ref> The Republic Bureau of Statistics published the full results in January 1991.<ref name="rezultati1990rzs">{{cite web |date=30 January 1991 |title=Izbori 1990.: Konačni rezultati izbora za predsednika republike i narodne poslanike |trans-title=1990 elections: Final results of the elections for the president of the republic and deputies |url=https://publikacije.stat.gov.rs/G1991/Pdf/G19916001.pdf |access-date=22 August 2023 |publisher=Republic Bureau of Statistics |language=sr |location=Belgrade |archive-date=25 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240925112142/https://publikacije.stat.gov.rs/G1991/Pdf/G19916001.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>{{rp|page=1}}

=== Presidential election ===
There were 7,033,610 citizens who were eligible to vote in the presidential elections.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=84|pages=}}<ref name="rezultati1990rzs" />{{rp|page=2}} Out of 9,013 voting stations, voting took place at 8,587 voting stations.<ref name="rezultati1990rzs" />{{rp|page=2}} At 22 voting stations, voting had to be repeated.<ref name="rezultati1990rzs" />{{rp|page=2}} The turnout for the election was 71%.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=84|pages=}} Milošević won the presidential election in a [[Landslide victory|landslide]],<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=84|pages=}}<ref name=":56">{{Cite news |last=Doder |first=Duško |date=12 December 1990 |title=Yugoslavia Breakup Seems a Step Closer / Serbian vote heightens nationalist tensions |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/302613271 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[San Francisco Chronicle]] |pages=A13|id={{ProQuest|302613271}} }}</ref> while Drašković only won 16% of the popular vote.<ref>{{Cite news |date=13 December 1990 |title=Serbian President re-elected, Communists take 87 seats |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/385562515 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[The Globe and Mail]] |pages=A17 |id={{ProQuest|385562515}}}}</ref> Observers were shocked, as they had expected Drašković to receive strong support.<ref name=":56" /> Đurić came third place, only winning just over 5% of the popular vote.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=83|pages=}} Milošević, Drašković, and Đurić received more votes than their parties in the parliamentary elections, respectively.<ref name="Martinov2000" />{{Rp|page=31}} [[Sulejman Ugljanin]] of the SDA received over 100,000 votes.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=84|pages=}} As Milošević won a majority of the popular vote, a second round was not held.<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=37|pages=}}<ref>{{Cite news |date=13 December 1990 |title=Briefly Communists score victory BELGRADE, Yugoslavia |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/261336748 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[San Antonio Express-News]] |pages=E9|id={{ProQuest|261336748}} }}</ref>

