=== Parliamentary election ===
There were 7,036,303 citizens who were eligible to vote in the first round of the parliamentary elections; in the second round, there were 4,352,573.<ref name=":60" />{{Rp|page=118}} In the first round, voting took place at 8,595 out of 9,013 voting stations, while in the second round, voting took place at 4,507 out of 4,906 voting stations.<ref name="rezultati1990rzs" />{{rp|page=|pages=4–5}} In the first round of the parliamentary election, 96 members were elected, while the rest, 154 members, were elected in the second round.<ref name=":25" /><ref name=":60" />{{Rp|page=118}} The voter turnout in the first round was reported to be 71%, the highest of any parliamentary election in Serbia since then.<ref name=":19" /><ref name=":60" />{{Rp|page=118}}<ref>{{Cite news |date=3 April 2022 |title=Infografika: Koliko građana je izlazilo na izbore od 1990. do danas |trans-title=Infographic: How many citizens have voted from 1990 to the present day |url=https://www.021.rs/story/Info/Srbija/301979/INFOGRAFIKA-Koliko-gradjana-je-izlazilo-na-izbore-od-1990-do-danas.html |access-date=10 July 2024 |work=021.rs |language=sr |archive-date=10 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710221512/https://www.021.rs/story/Info/Srbija/301979/INFOGRAFIKA-Koliko-gradjana-je-izlazilo-na-izbore-od-1990-do-danas.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In the second round, the turnout was 48%.<ref name=":60" />{{Rp|page=118}} While the turnout in other Yugoslav republics was higher, the turnout of 71% in the first round largely resulted from Kosovo Albanians boycotting the election.<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=|pages=24–25}}

The election resulted in a [[landslide victory]] for Milošević's SPS, winning 194 out of 250 seats in the National Assembly.<ref name=":57">{{Cite news |date=26 December 1990 |title=Ex-Communists trounce opposition in Serbian election |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/282971809 |access-date=14 July 2024 |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |pages=3|id={{ProQuest|282971809}} }}</ref> Despite not winning the popular vote, SPS received 78% of seats in the National Assembly due to the first-past-the-post system.<ref name="Martinov2000" />{{Rp|page=26}}<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=74}} The SPO, in second place, won 19 seats, while the DS, in third place, won 7 seats.<ref name=":19" /> Minority parties won 14 seats, and independents won 8.<ref name=":22" /><ref name=":57" /> Out of all minority parties, the [[Democratic Fellowship of Vojvodina Hungarians]] (VMDK) won the most seats, 8 in total.<ref name="rezultati1990rzs" />{{rp|page=6|pages=}} Liberal parties, such as DS, and social-democratic parties, such as SDPJ, did not garner a lot of support compared to other post-communist countries.<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=|pages=30}} Only two women were elected to the National Assembly.<ref name=":202" />{{Rp|page=624}}

Goati argued that the SPS won due to Serbia's economic underdevelopment and its electoral system.<ref name=":59" />{{Rp|page=|pages=12–13}} He also argued that state-controlled media campaigns against opposition parties also helped SPS in the campaign.<ref name=":59" />{{Rp|page=13|pages=}} Political scientist Srećko Mihailović argued that the mixture of nationalism and socialism resulted in the SPS victory.<ref name=":61" />{{Rp|page=6}}

Voting was repeated at 3 voting stations on 16 December.<ref name=":60" />{{Rp|page=118}} A [[1991 Rakovica I by-election|by-election]] was held in June 1991 due to the death of [[Miodrag Bulatović]], elected with the nomination of SPS.<ref name=":33" /> Šešelj of SRS, Pekić of DS, Jovan Marjanović of SPO, and Radoš Karaklajić of SPS were the election's candidates.<ref name=":33" /> Despite the SPS nominating its own candidate, Milošević gave campaign infrastructure to Šešelj instead.<ref name=":33" /> Šešelj was eventually elected a member in the second round.<ref name=":33" />

