=== Voter demographics ===
A majority of the working class voted for SPS in the 1990 election.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=78}}<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Vuković |first1=Đorđe |url=https://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/Oko%20izbora%2018.pdf |title=Oko izbora 18: Predsednički i parlamentarni izbori maj 2012. |last2=Žegarac |first2=Jelena |last3=Čolović |first3=Ivo |date=2012 |publisher=[[CeSID]] |isbn=978-86-83491-52-0 |location=Belgrade |pages=87 |language=sr |trans-title=Oko izbora 18: May 2012 presidential and parliamentary elections |archive-date=21 June 2024 |access-date=15 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240621025856/https://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/Oko%20izbora%2018.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> Academics Dragomir Pantić and Zoran Pavlović analysed the demographics of the three major parties during the 1990 elections. According to them, SPS voters were characterised by authoritarianism, nationalism, irreligiousness, and nostalgia for socialism; DS voters were characterised by anti-authoritarianism, nationalism, irreligiosity, and rare nostalgia for socialism; and SPO voters were characterised by mild authoritarianism, nationalism, religiousity, and an absence of nostalgia for socialism.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Pantić |first1=Dragomir |title=Političke stranke i birači u državama bivše Jugoslavije |last2=Pavlović |first2=Zoran |date=2006 |publisher=[[Friedrich Ebert Foundation]], [[Institute of Social Sciences (Serbia)|Institute for Social Sciences]] |isbn=978-86-83767-16-8 |location=Belgrade |pages=52–53 |language=sr |trans-title=Political parties and voters in the countries of the former Yugoslavia |chapter=Stranačke pristalice i komponente političke kulture u Srbiji |trans-chapter=Party supporters and components of political culture in Serbia}}</ref> Political scientist {{Ill|Srbobran Branković|sr|Srbobran Branković}} said that SPS voters included the elderly, those with lower education, such as pensioners, housewives, and peasants, and workers spanning from the unqualified to highly qualified workers. Regarding the opposition, their voters included younger generations and those with higher education, such as students, professors, intellectuals, and private entrepreneurs.<ref name=":70">{{Cite journal |last=Branković |first=Srbobran |date=1992 |title=O paradoksalnosti izbornih rezultata |trans-title=On the paradoxical nature of election results |journal=[[Gledišta]] |language=sr |volume=33 |issue=1 |issn=0017-1166}}</ref>{{Rp|page=62}} According to Martinov, 28% of SPS voters advocated for an authoritarian regime, and 33% espoused [[social justice]].<ref name="Martinov2000" />{{Rp|page=28}} According to the Institute of Social Sciences, SPS voters were the most authoritarian, while DS voters were the most libertarian.<ref name=":72">{{Cite book |last1=Joković Pantelić |first1=Milica |title=Demokratske promene u Srbiji |last2=Bašić |first2=Goran |date=2023 |publisher=[[Institute of Social Sciences (Belgrade)|Institute of Social Sciences]] |isbn=978-86-7093-265-4 |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=Democratic changes in Serbia}}</ref>{{Rp|page=157}} Regarding presidential candidates, supporters of Milošević espoused the highest amounts of authoritarianism, while supporters of Đurić and Šešelj were the most libertarian.<ref name=":72" />{{Rp|page=|pages=147, 157}}

Mihailović, who analysed the Institute of Social Studies and Centre for Political Research polls, saw that SPS voters were either from suburban parts or villages and mostly from the [[Central Serbia]] region. Most SPS voters were elderly and elementary school educated, and a majority of them identified themselves on the left-wing part of the political spectrum. Most SPO voters, primarily male, high-school educated, and from middle-sized cities in Central Serbia, identified as politically right-leaning, with 26% considering themselves centrist. DS voters were young, lived in cities, and were highly educated. Most DS voters saw themselves in the political centre.<ref name=":44" />{{rp|pages=28–30|page=}}

The Institute of Social Studies and Centre for Political Research poll conducted in December also listed the influence of voter results in the election; 28.7% said that radio had little influence on them, while 12.7% said that it had "a great deal" of influence on them.<ref name="IDN_December1990" /> On the other hand, television impacted most voters, while the press had little influence on the outcome of their voting.<ref name="IDN_December1990" /> Most of the voters had already known who they would vote for since the beginning, with 16.4% also stating that they formed their decision during the election campaign.<ref name="IDN_December1990" /> Regarding the political positioning of voters, 28.4% identified as centrist, 21.6% as mildly left, 11.1% as mildly right, 9.6% as centre-left, 6.4% as extreme-left, 5.6% as centre-right, and 2.8% as extreme-right.<ref name="IDN_December1990" /> Most of the voters favoured closer relations with the [[European Economic Community]], while 28.4% opted for a strictly neutral policy.<ref name="IDN_December1990" />

== Reactions ==
Šešelj was the first candidate to congratulate Milošević once the results were announced.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=75}} Milošević was also felicitated by Ante Marković and [[Franjo Tuđman]], the newly elected president of Croatia.<ref name=":81">{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=23 December 2010 |title=Milošević je predsednik, živeo drugi krug |trans-title=Milošević is the president, long live the second round |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/milosevic-je-predsednik-ziveo-drugi-krug/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr |archive-date=18 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240718201148/https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/milosevic-je-predsednik-ziveo-drugi-krug/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Đurić claimed that Milošević did not win 50% of the popular vote and that the results from opposition candidates were subtracted from and added on top of Milošević's result.<ref name=":81" />

''Vreme'' reported that opposition leaders were "in a state of shock for about 20 hours" (''gotovo dvadeset sati bili u šoku'').<ref name=":82" /> Drašković said after the elections that "citizens voted for restraints, for Bolshevism, for the past, for darkness, and for shame" (''građani glasali za stege, za boljševizam, za prošlost, za mrak i za sramotu''), and initially said that SPO might not accept the results.<ref name=":33" /><ref name=":53" /> He also claimed that he had wished for such exact results.<ref name=":81" /> Đinđić described the voters as not having heard of the [[fall of the Berlin Wall]].<ref name=":82" /> [[Dragan Jovanović (Green Party politician)|Dragan Jovanović]], the president of ZS, said that his party was disappointed and that "the people got what they deserved" (''narod je dobio ono što je zaslužio'').<ref name=":81" /> [[Nebojša Popov]] from UJDI compared the election to the pre-World War II Weimar Germany elections.<ref name=":33" /> [[Dušan Mihajlović (politician)|Dušan Mihajlović]] of ND was more optimistic, saying that "the battle for true democracy has just begun" (''bitka za pravu demokratiju je tek počela'').<ref name=":81" /> Albanian LDK rejected recognising the legitimacy of the elections.<ref>{{Cite news |date=14 December 1990 |title=Ethnic Albanians reject Serbia's first multi-party polls |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1990/12/14/Ethnic-Albanians-reject-Serbias-first-multi-party-polls/7691661150800/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[United Press International]] |archive-date=25 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230325175402/https://www.upi.com/Archives/1990/12/14/Ethnic-Albanians-reject-Serbias-first-multi-party-polls/7691661150800/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

The poll conducted by the Institute for Political Studies in Belgrade saw 55% of the respondents satisfied with the results of the presidential election, with 22% also being neither satisfied nor unsatisfied, while 21% said that they were unsatisfied with the results.<ref name="IDN_December1990" /> Regarding the parliamentary elections, 37% were satisfied, 33% were unsatisfied, and 28% were neither satisfied nor unsatisfied.<ref name="IDN_December1990" /> 26.5% of the respondents said that the election would somewhat influence their future lives, and 22% said that it would not at all influence their future lives; 32% did not know.<ref name="IDN_December1990" />

== Aftermath ==
=== Government formation ===
[[File:Dragutin Zelenović 1990s.JPG|thumb|upright=0.8|alt=Dragutin Zelenović talking with Borisav Jović in the National Assembly of Serbia|[[Dragutin Zelenović]] was elected prime minister of Serbia after the 1990 election]]
The National Assembly was constituted on 11 January 1991.<ref>{{Cite news |date=9 December 2010 |title=Dve decenije višestranačja u Srbiji |trans-title=Two decades of multi-partyism in Serbia |url=https://rtv.rs/rsn/drustvo/dve-decenije-visestranacja-u-srbiji_227349.html |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[Radio Television of Vojvodina]] |language=sr |archive-date=20 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240720132421/https://rtv.rs/rsn/drustvo/dve-decenije-visestranacja-u-srbiji_227349.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Since 10 [[member of parliament|members of parliament]] (MPs) were needed to form a parliamentary group, only the SPS and SPO were able to do so.<ref name=":73" />{{Rp|page=|pages=135–137}} DS, VMDK, and several independents united to form their own group.<ref name=":73" />{{Rp|page=137|pages=}} [[Slobodan Unković]] was elected as the president of the National Assembly.<ref name=":25" /><ref name=":83">{{Cite news |last=Torov |first=Milica |date=5 December 1997 |title=Parlament Srbije od demokratije do autoritarnosti |trans-title=The Parliament of Serbia from democracy to authoritarianism |url=http://www.yurope.com/nasa-borba/arhiva/Dec97/0512/0512_20.HTM |access-date=20 July 2024 |work=[[Naša borba]] |language=sr |archive-date=20 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240720132612/http://www.yurope.com/nasa-borba/arhiva/Dec97/0512/0512_20.HTM |url-status=live }}</ref> Unković remained in that position until his resignation in June, stating that he was worn out and wanted to return to the academic sphere.<ref name=":83" /> He was succeeded by [[Aleksandar Bakočević]], who remained in that position until the [[1992 Serbian general election|1992 elections]].<ref name=":83" />

With the conclusion of the 1990 elections, Serbia turned from a one-party system to a [[competitive authoritarian]] multi-party system.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Eibl |first1=Otto |title=Thirty Years of Political Campaigning in Central and Eastern Europe |last2=Gregor |first2=Miloš |last3=Atlagić |first3=Siniša |last4=Vučićević |first4=Dušan |date=2019 |publisher=[[Palgrave Macmillan]] |isbn=978-3-030-27692-8 |location=Brno, Czech Republic |pages=325}}</ref> Sotirović also said that political scientists considered the system to be authoritarian during the process of the dissolution of Yugoslavia;<ref name=":67" />{{Rp|pages=7–8}} Goati defined it as a [[dominant-party system]].<ref name=":73" />{{Rp|page=21}} Considering that SPS obtained a supermajority in the National Assembly, the [[Cabinet of Dragutin Zelenović|next government of Serbia]] was led solely by SPS.<ref name=":65">{{Cite book |last1=Mihailović |first1=Srećko |url=https://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/Oko%20izbora%2017.pdf |title=Oko izbora 17: Parlamentarni izbori u Republici Srbiji, 11. maj 2008. godine |last2=Stojiljković |first2=Zoran |date=2008 |publisher=[[CeSID]] |isbn=978-86-83491-47-6 |location=Belgrade |language=sr |trans-title=Oko izbora 17: Parliamentary elections in the Republic of Serbia on 11 May 2008 |archive-date=21 June 2024 |access-date=15 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240621025744/https://www.cesid.rs/pdfovi/Oko%20izbora%2017.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Rp|page=114}}<ref name=":91">{{Cite thesis |last=Ružić |first=Stefan |title=Izborne jedinice u većinskim i proporcionalnim izborima [Election constituencies in first-past-the-post and proportional elections] |date=2015 |degree=Master |publisher=[[University of Niš]] |chapter= |page=}}</ref>{{Rp|page=68}} [[Dragutin Zelenović]] was elected prime minister of Serbia by the National Assembly in February 1991.<ref name=":65" />{{Rp|page=117}} He served in the office until December 1991, when his government was replaced by [[Cabinet of Radoman Božović|the cabinet]] led by [[Radoman Božović]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=5 March 2026 |title=Pitajte novčanik za koga ste glasali |trans-title=Ask your wallet who you voted for |url=https://vreme.com/projekat/pitajte-novcanik-za-koga-ste-glasali/ |access-date=2026-06-18 |website=[[Vreme]] |language=sr}}</ref> Both governments included SPS and independent ministers.<ref name=":65" />{{Rp|page=117}}

=== 1991 protests ===
{{Further|1991 protests in Belgrade}}
The first major anti-government protest after the 1990 elections was held on 9 March 1991 in Belgrade.<ref name=":92">{{Cite news |last=Kojić |first=Nikola |date=18 February 2020 |title=Izbori 1992: Prvi Šešeljev milion, Arkan u parlamentu i manjinska vlada SPS |trans-title=1992 elections: Šešelj's first million, Arkan in the Parliament and SPS minority government |url=https://n1info.rs/vesti/a570469-izbori-1992-prvi-seseljev-milion-arkan-u-parlamentu-i-manjinska-vlada-sps/ |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[N1 (TV channel)|N1]] |language=sr |archive-date=20 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230120233037/https://n1info.rs/vesti/a570469-izbori-1992-prvi-seseljev-milion-arkan-u-parlamentu-i-manjinska-vlada-sps/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The main organiser of the protest was SPO, though other opposition parties also took part.<ref name=":92" /> The protest was organised in response to the media's treatment of the opposition, with SPO calling for the resignation of senior officials of RTB.<ref name=":92" /><ref name=":93">{{Cite news |date=9 March 2024 |title=U Beogradu, 9. marta 1991. održane prve masovne demonstracije protiv Miloševićevog režima |trans-title=In Belgrade, on 9 March 1991, the first mass demonstrations against the Milošević regime were held |url=https://beta.rs/content/201463-u-beogradu-9-marta-1991-odrzane-prve-masovne-demonstracije-protiv-milosevicevog-rezima |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[Novinska agencija Beta]] |language=sr}}</ref> RTB previously alleged in February 1991 that SPO cooperated "with the far pro-fascistic and pro-Ustaša Croatia" (''sa krajnjom profašističkom i proustaškom Hrvatskom'').<ref name=":94">{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=9 March 2011 |title=Dan kada se zaljuljao Miloševićev režim (1. deo) |trans-title=The day when Milosevic's regime almost collapsed (part 1) |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/9-mart-dve-decenije-1-deo/ |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr}}</ref> Drašković, alongside Đinđić, Mićunović, [[Vesna Pešić]] of SRSJ, and [[Milan Paroški]] of NS,<ref name=":90" /><ref name=":95">{{Cite news |last=Vasić |first=Miloš |date=9 March 2011 |title=Kako je bilo i urbane legende |trans-title=How it was and urban legends |url=https://vreme.com/vreme/kako-je-bilo-i-urbane-legende/ |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[Vreme]] |language=sr}}</ref> expressed his demands from the balcony of the [[National Theatre in Belgrade|National Theatre]],<ref name=":90" /><ref name=":94" /> As the Serbian government sought JNA intervention, [[Borisav Jović]] convinced other constituent republics by claiming that demonstrators were attempting to seize the [[Yugoslav Ministry of Defence Building|Yugoslav General Staff]] building and that the police had been dispersed and were unable to manage the protest.<ref name=":95" /> Slovenia, Croatia, Macedonia, and Bosnia and Herzegovina opposed JNA's intervention in the protest, while Serbia, Montenegro, Kosovo, and Vojvodina were in favour.<ref name=":95" /> Kadijević eventually allowed JNA to intervene in the protests, and tanks were brought to the streets of Belgrade for the first time since 1944.<ref name=":95" />

The intervention of JNA and the police left one demonstrator and one policeman dead.<ref name=":90" /><ref name=":92" /> About 200 demonstrators were left injured.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=82}}<ref name=":93" /> In response to the protest, the government banned Radio B92, censured Studio B, and detained Drašković.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=82}}<ref name=":92" /> Milošević declared that the demonstrators were the "forces of chaos and violence" (''silama haosa i nasilja'').<ref name=":90" /> According to the reports of the police, about 40,000 demonstrators were present at the 9 March protest.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=81}}<ref name=":95" /> A day later, another protest was organised, this time with students also taking part in the protest.<ref name=":90" /><ref name=":92" /> A group of 5,000 students marched from [[Studentski Grad, Belgrade|Studentski Grad]] towards [[Terazije]], but were stopped and beaten by the police at the [[Branko's Bridge]].<ref name=":92" /> The police eventually allowed the students to continue towards Terazije.<ref name=":92" /> JNA withdrew from the streets on the same day.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=|page=82}} Amidst the protests, the National Assembly held a session, while the Presidency of Yugoslavia decided not to proclaim a [[state of emergency]] in Serbia.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=24 March 2011 |title=Svi, svi – do pobede! (8. deo) |trans-title=Everyone, everyone – to victory! (Part 8) |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/svi-svi-do-pobede-8-deo/ |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr |archive-date=28 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240728134259/https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/svi-svi-do-pobede-8-deo/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The protests were held continuously until 14 March, when the government conceded to the demands of the organisers.<ref name=":92" /> [[Dušan Mitević]], the director of RTB, was sacked, Radio B92 and Studio B were allowed to continue their operations, and all detainees, including Drašković, were set free.<ref name=":29" />{{Rp|pages=84–85|page=}}<ref name=":90" /><ref name=":92" /> The editorial policy of RTB, however, remained the same and continued to show SPS in a positive light while criticising the opposition.<ref name=":92" /> In late March, UOS organised a protest, demanding snap elections, a change of government, and the investigation of 9–14 March protests.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Dedeić |first=Siniša |date=29 March 2011 |title="Ko u slobodu veruje, taj je i donosi" (11. deo) |trans-title="Whoever believes in freedom brings it" (part 11) |url=https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/ko-u-slobodu-veruje-taj-je-i-donosi-11-deo-foto-galerija/ |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[Istinomer]] |language=sr |archive-date=28 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240728151114/https://www.istinomer.rs/amnezija/ko-u-slobodu-veruje-taj-je-i-donosi-11-deo-foto-galerija/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Yugoslav Wars ===
{{Further|Yugoslav Wars|Breakup of Yugoslavia}}
The process of the [[breakup of Yugoslavia]] escalated after the Serbian elections, with Milošević continuing to espouse conspiracy theories and allegations, such as that [[Warren Zimmermann]], the last U.S. ambassador to Yugoslavia, was an agent of the [[Central Intelligence Agency]], that Slovenia, Austria, and Hungary sought to re-establish the [[Austria-Hungary|Austria-Hungarian Empire]], and that there was an international plot to destroy Serbia.<ref name=":86">{{Cite news |last=Gagnon, Jr. |first=V.P. |date=1 June 1991 |title=Yugoslavia: Prospects for Stability |url=https://www.foreignaffairs.com/articles/europe/1991-06-01/yugoslavia-prospects-stability |access-date=27 July 2024 |work=[[Foreign Affairs]] |archive-date=24 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210824110922/https://www.foreignaffairs.com/articles/europe/1991-06-01/yugoslavia-prospects-stability |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":87">{{Cite book |last1=Ramet |first1=Sabrina P. |title=Serbia Since 1989: Politics and Society under Milosevic and After |last2=Pavlaković |first2=Vjeran I. |date=2006 |publisher=[[University of Washington Press]] |isbn=978-0-295-98650-0}}</ref>{{Rp|page=125}} The ''[[Foreign Affairs]]'' reported that none of the six constituent republics paid their full share of tax revenues to the government of Yugoslavia and that all republics, except Serbia and Montenegro, prioritised their sovereignty over the federation.<ref name=":86" /> The ethnic nationalist sentiment was also adopted by all leaders of Yugoslav constituent republics; Milošević portrayed the Serbs in Croatia as being on "the verge of extermination".<ref name=":86" /><ref name=":87" />{{Rp|page=3}} Additionally, with the support of Milošević, the [[SAO Krajina]] was established by Serbs in Croatia.<ref name=":86" />

Croatia and Slovenia moderated their stances in early 1991 mainly due to economic factors; the European Economic Community and [[International Monetary Fund]] both wanted to cooperate only with a united Yugoslavia.<ref name=":86" /> Because Tuđman still opted for a confederal Yugoslavia, he faced criticism within the right-wing factions of his party, the [[Croatian Democratic Union]].<ref name=":86" /> [[Janez Drnovšek]], who served as Slovenia's representative in the Yugoslav presidency, also said that Slovenia's independence could not be achieved unilaterally.<ref name=":86" /> Milošević eventually moderated his stances in March 1991 due to the protests.<ref name=":86" /> Despite this, Serb paramilitaries advanced and the [[Croatian War of Independence]] began in the same month.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Vladisavljević |first=Anja |date=31 March 2021 |title=Hrvatska: Obeležena 30. godišnjica operacije "Plitvice" |trans-title=Croatia: 30th anniversary of operation "Plitvice" marked |url=https://balkaninsight.com/sr/2021/03/31/hrvatska-obelezena-30-godisnjica-operacije-plitvice/ |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[Balkan Insight]] |language=sh |archive-date=29 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240729085444/https://balkaninsight.com/sr/2021/03/31/hrvatska-obelezena-30-godisnjica-operacije-plitvice/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Ramet |first=Sabrina P. |title=The Three Yugoslavias: State-building and Legitimation, 1918–2005 |date=2006 |publisher=[[Indiana University Press]] |isbn=978-0-253-34656-8 |location=Bloomington, Indiana |pages=384–385}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Engelberg |first=Stephen |date=3 March 1991 |title=Belgrade Sends Troops to Croatia Town |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1991/03/03/world/belgrade-sends-troops-to-croatia-town.html |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]] |pages=3 |archive-date=2 October 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131002222925/http://www.nytimes.com/1991/03/03/world/belgrade-sends-troops-to-croatia-town.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[Republic of Serbian Krajina]] was later established on the territory of Croatia in December 1991.<ref name=":87" />{{Rp|page=14, 55}}<ref>{{Cite book |last=Bugajski |first=Janusz |title=Ethnic Politics in Eastern Europe: A Guide to Nationality Policies, Organizations, and Parties |date=1994 |publisher=[[M. E. Sharpe]] |isbn=978-1-56324-282-3 |pages=63}}</ref> Later in April 1992, the [[Bosnian War]] also began, after Bosnia and Herzegovina proclaimed independence.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Taylor |first=Alan |date=13 April 2012 |title=20 Years Since The Bosnian War |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/photo/2012/04/20-years-since-the-bosnian-war/100278/ |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[The Atlantic]] |archive-date=28 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240728151112/https://www.theatlantic.com/photo/2012/04/20-years-since-the-bosnian-war/100278/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Bose |first=Sumantra |title=Contested Lands |date=2009 |publisher=[[Harvard University Press]] |isbn=978-0-674-02856-2 |pages=124}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Hammond |first=Philip |title=Framing Post-Cold War Conflicts: The Media and International Intervention |date=2007 |publisher=[[Manchester University Press]] |isbn=978-0-7190-7696-1 |pages=51}}</ref>

After the announcement that the European Economic Community would recognise Slovenia and Croatia, Milošević declared the formation of a "third Yugoslavia" which included Serbia, Montenegro, and Krajina.<ref name=":87" />{{Rp|page=55}} In February 1992, Serbia and Montenegro only agreed to continue existing as part of the same state; the [[Federal Republic of Yugoslavia]] was then created in April, and the SFRY formally ceased to exist.<ref name=":87" />{{Rp|page=56}} The new state was isolated and suspended from a number of international organisations, such as the United Nations.<ref name=":87" />{{Rp|page=56}}<ref>{{Cite news |last=Doyle |first=Leonard |date=22 September 1992 |title=UN suspends former Yugoslavia |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/europe/un-suspends-former-yugoslavia-1553074.html |access-date=27 July 2024 |work=[[The Independent]] |archive-date=26 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200726142309/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/europe/un-suspends-former-yugoslavia-1553074.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The United Nations also [[International sanctions against the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia|sanctioned FR Yugoslavia]] due to the wars in Croatia and Bosnia.<ref name=":91" />{{Rp|page=69}}<ref>{{Cite news |last=Lewsi |first=Paul |date=31 May 1992 |title=U.N. Votes 13–0 For Embargo on Trade With Yugoslavia; Air Travel and Oil Curbed |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1992/05/31/world/un-votes-13-0-for-embargo-on-trade-with-yugoslavia-air-travel-and-oil-curbed.html |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]] |pages=1 |archive-date=26 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200726221329/https://www.nytimes.com/1992/05/31/world/un-votes-13-0-for-embargo-on-trade-with-yugoslavia-air-travel-and-oil-curbed.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Bideleux |first1=Robert |title=The Balkans: A Post-Communist History |last2=Jeffries |first2=Ian |date=2007 |publisher=[[Routledge]] |isbn=978-1-134-58327-0 |pages=1977}}</ref>

=== Snap elections ===
{{Further|1992 Serbian early elections referendum|1992 Serbian general election}}
Dissatisfied with the results of the 1990 elections and believing they had greater public support, the opposition called for the government to hold a snap election.<ref name=":91" />{{Rp|page=69}} With the 1991 protests, the beginning of the Yugoslav Wars, the creation of FR Yugoslavia, and the introduction of sanctions on the newly established state, all parties agreed that snap elections should be held on all levels.<ref name=":91" />{{Rp|page=69}} Additionally, upon the proclamation of FR Yugoslavia, the government organised [[May 1992 Yugoslavian parliamentary election|parliamentary elections for the Federal Parliament in May]].<ref name=":92" /> The opposition boycotted the election in protest of the election laws and the unfair treatment of the media against the opposition.<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=|pages=84}}<ref name=":92" /> The government held a referendum in October 1992 on whether snap elections should be called in Serbia.<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=85|pages=}} The referendum was approved by the voters, though the turnout was only 46% of all registered voters, which was not enough for the decision to be considered legally valid.<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=85|pages=}} The government of Serbia then proposed a package of reforms, which the National Assembly adopted shortly after the referendum.<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=85|pages=}}<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Bakočević |first=Aleksandar |date=22 October 1992 |title=Zaključak o prevremenim izborima na svim novima u Republici Srbiji |url=http://bpp.uzzpro.gov.rs/(X(1)S(ezlzjf55vdcwh1iybweuw0jj))/Register.aspx?oznaka=11110 |journal=Official Gazette of the Republic of Serbia |volume=76 |pages=2538 |issn=0353-8389}}</ref> Snap elections were then scheduled for 20 December 1992.<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=86|pages=}}<ref name=":92" />

It was debated whether the first-past-the-post system should stay or if Serbia should adopt the proportional representation system.<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=85|pages=}}<ref name=":91" />{{Rp|page=69}} The government initially wanted the first-past-the-post system to stay.<ref name=":92" /> The election laws were ultimately amended and the proportional representation system was established; the number of constituencies was also lowered from 250 to 9 as a compromise.<ref name=":62" />{{Rp|page=85|pages=}}<ref name=":91" />{{Rp|page=69}} Under the new electoral system, the Belgrade constituency elected 46 MPs, Novi Sad elected 32 MPs, Kragujevac elected 29 MPs, Leskovac elected 25 MPs, Zrenjanin, Niš, Priština, and Užice all elected 24 MPs each, and Smederevo elected 22 MPs.<ref name=":91" />{{Rp|page=|pages=69–70}}

== Legacy ==
The Radio Television of Serbia published a documentary about the 1990 elections in 2020.<ref name=":66">{{Cite news |date=8 December 2020 |title=Sricanje demokratije – izbori 1990. |trans-title=Spelling democracy – 1990 elections |url=https://www.rts.rs/lat/tv/rts1/4176371/sricanje-demokratije---izbori-1990.html |access-date=15 July 2024 |work=[[Radio Television of Serbia]] |language=sr}}</ref> The documentary featured politicians who took part in the 1990 elections, as well as political scientists and researchers.<ref name=":66" /> In the documentary, lawyer and SPS member {{Ill|Ratko Marković|sr|Ratko Marković}}, who wrote the 1990 constitution, also explained the reason why multi-party elections were held after the constitutional referendum.<ref name=":96">{{Cite news |last=Veljković |first=Vladimir |date=22 April 2021 |title=Nestabilna Srbija |trans-title=Unstable Serbia |url=https://pescanik.net/nestabilna-srbija/ |access-date=28 July 2024 |work=[[Peščanik]] |language=sr}}</ref> "It was necessary to act quickly, primarily because of the situation regarding the autonomous provinces in our country, primarily Kosovo and Metohija" (''Trebalo je brzo delovati, pre svega zbog situacije u vezi sa autonomnim pokrajinama kod nas, pre svega Kosovo i Metohija''), Marković said.<ref name=":96" />

''Prizvan i pozvan'' (Invoked), a documentary on the 1990 elections was released in 2022. Produced by Luka Papić and Srđa Vučo, the documentary included comedy elements about the elections.<ref name=":510">{{Cite news |date=8 September 2023 |title=Srđa Vučo, reditelj i producent: Politička komedija sa tragičnm posljedicama |trans-title=Srđa Vučo, director and producer: A political comedy with tragic consequences |url=https://www.monitor.co.me/srdja-vuco-reditelj-i-producent-politicka-komedija-sa-tragicnim-posljedicama/ |access-date=30 July 2024 |work=[[Monitor (magazine)|Monitor]] |language=sr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Pavičić |first=Jurica |date=14 May 2023 |title=Nekadašnji junaci političkih izbora u Srbiji: samo jednom od likova filma nacionalizam nije pojeo mozak |trans-title=Former heroes of political elections in Serbia: only one of the film's characters was not brainwashed by nationalism |url=https://www.jutarnji.hr/kultura/film-i-televizija/nekadasnji-junaci-politickih-izbora-u-srbiji-samo-jednom-od-likova-filma-nacionalizam-nije-pojeo-mozak-15335627 |access-date=31 July 2023 |work=[[Jutarnji list]] |language=hr |archive-date=23 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230523180312/https://www.jutarnji.hr/kultura/film-i-televizija/nekadasnji-junaci-politickih-izbora-u-srbiji-samo-jednom-od-likova-filma-nacionalizam-nije-pojeo-mozak-15335627 |url-status=live }}</ref> The documentary featured Ćuić, Mladenović, Jovanović of ZS, as well as parliamentary candidate Savo Nešković of the [[Great Rock 'n' Roll Party]].<ref name=":410">{{Cite news |last=Koprivica |first=Jelena |date=18 July 2024 |title="Svaka budala u Srbiji može da osnuje političku stranku": Kako je film o prvim višestranačkim izborima razotkrio laž |trans-title="Every fool in Serbia can form a political party": How the film about the first multi-party elections exposed the lie |url=https://nova.rs/kultura/svaka-budala-u-srbiji-moze-da-osnuje-politicku-stranku-kako-je-film-o-prvim-visestranackim-izborima-razotkrio-laz/ |access-date=31 July 2024 |work=NOVA portal |language=sr |archive-date=31 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240731120220/https://nova.rs/kultura/svaka-budala-u-srbiji-moze-da-osnuje-politicku-stranku-kako-je-film-o-prvim-visestranackim-izborima-razotkrio-laz/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The documentary was screened at several festivals, including the [[International Documentary Film Festival Amsterdam]], [[Subversive Festival]], and [[Palić European Film Festival]].<ref name=":410" /> It received the "Audience Award for the Best European Documentary Film" at the 2023 {{Ill|Beldocs Festival|sr|Beldocs — Međunarodni festival dokumentarnog filma}}.<ref>{{Cite news |date=17 May 2023 |title=Filmu "Prizvan i pozvan" nagrada publike za najbolji evropski dokumentarni film na ovogodišnjem Beldoksu |trans-title=The film "Invoked" won the audience award for the best European documentary film at this year's Beldocs |url=https://www.euronews.rs/kultura/aktuelno-iz-kulture/87725/filmu-prizvan-i-pozvan-nagrada-publike-za-najbolji-evropski-dokumentarni-film-na-ovogodisnjem-beldoksu/vest |access-date=31 July 2024 |work=[[Euronews]] |language=sr |archive-date=31 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240731120217/https://www.euronews.rs/kultura/aktuelno-iz-kulture/87725/filmu-prizvan-i-pozvan-nagrada-publike-za-najbolji-evropski-dokumentarni-film-na-ovogodisnjem-beldoksu/vest |url-status=live }}</ref>

